export const c11Phy3HtmlOverview = "<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 16px;\">\n\n  <!-- CHAPTER TITLE BANNER -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px; text-align: center;\">\n    <h1 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 24px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Unit III: Laws of Motion (07 Marks)\n    </h1>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 0; font-size: 15px; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Comprehensive Reference Textbook Guide &bull; JKBOSE / CBSE Official Syllabus Alignment\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- QUICK GLOSSARY & BASIC DEFINITIONS CARD -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      ⚡ Quick Glossary & Fundamental Operational Quantities\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; font-size: 14px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 4px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Force (F):</b> External agency (push or pull) that changes or tends to change the state of rest or uniform motion. SI Unit: <b>Newton (N)</b> &bull; Dimensions: <b>[M L T<sup>-2</sup>]</b>.\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 4px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Linear Momentum (p):</b> Quantity of motion contained in a body; \u000bec{p} = m\u000bec{v}. SI Unit: <b>kg m s<sup>-1</sup></b> &bull; Dimensions: <b>[M L T<sup>-1</sup>]</b>.\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 4px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Impulse (J):</b> Product of average force and duration of impact; \u000bec{J} = \u000bec{F}_{\text{avg}} Delta t = Delta\u000bec{p}. SI Unit: <b>N s (or kg m s<sup>-1</sup>)</b> &bull; Dimensions: <b>[M L T<sup>-1</sup>]</b>.\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 10px 12px; border-left: 3px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 4px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Coefficient of Friction (&mu;):</b> Ratio of limiting friction to normal reaction; mu = f_L / N. <b>Dimensionless & Unitless [M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]</b>.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 1 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF8A65; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    1. Intuitive Concept of Force & Galileo's Historical Insight\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    In classical Newtonian mechanics, our intuitive understanding of <b>force</b> originates from muscular sensation—pushing a heavy crate, pulling a cart, stretching a spring, or kicking a football. Historically, the Greek philosopher Aristotle asserted that a constant external force is continuously required to keep a body in uniform rectilinear motion (\"Aristotelian fallacy\"). Aristotle argued that an arrow in flight continues forward only because air rushes behind it to push it. If the force ceased, the body would naturally come to rest.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    <b>Galileo Galilei</b> shattered this misconception through ingenious thought experiments with double inclined planes. Galileo observed that a marble released from rest on a smooth inclined plane rolls down, accelerating, and ascends an opposing inclined plane to almost the exact same vertical height from which it was released. When Galileo progressively decreased the inclination angle of the opposing plane, the marble traveled farther and farther along the slope to achieve that same vertical altitude.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    In the limiting case where the opposing slope is reduced to horizontal, Galileo deduced that if all opposing forces such as friction and air resistance could be eliminated entirely, the marble would roll along the horizontal surface forever with constant speed without requiring any pushing force! Therefore, Galileo concluded: <i>An external force is required not to maintain uniform motion, but to alter the state of motion (i.e. to produce acceleration or deceleration).</i>\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(14, 165, 233, 0.06); border-left: 4px solid #0EA5E9; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #38BDF8; font-size: 18px;\">💡</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #38BDF8; font-size: 15px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">REAL-WORLD INTUITION &bull; Ice Hockey Puck on Low-Friction Surface</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">A hockey puck struck across rough concrete halts in a couple of meters due to large friction. The same puck struck with identical effort across polished rink ice slides for dozens of meters. On an idealized frictionless surface in deep outer space, the puck would glide forever at constant velocity without burning a single drop of fuel.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF8A65; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    2. Inertia & Newton's First Law of Motion\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    <b>Inertia</b> (from the Latin word <i>iners</i>, meaning idle or sluggish) is that inherent physical property of all material bodies by virtue of which they resist any change in their existing state of rest or of uniform motion along a straight line. The mass of a body is the direct quantitative measure of its inertia: a massive locomotive possesses enormously greater inertia than a bicycle and requires vastly larger net forces to change its speed.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #FF8A65; color: #121212; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px;\">Newton's First Law of Motion</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Every physical body continues in its state of rest or of uniform motion along a straight line, unless it is compelled to change that state by an unbalanced external force impressed upon it.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFCC80; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">—</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFCC80; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    Newton's First Law provides two vital conceptual insights:\n  </p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">(i) Qualitative Definition of Force:</b> Force is defined as that external push, pull, or interaction which alters or tends to alter the natural state of rest or uniform rectilinear motion of a body.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">(ii) Definition of Inertial Frame of Reference:</b> An inertial frame is any reference frame in which Newton's First Law holds true without invoking artificial pseudo-forces. Any frame at rest or moving with constant velocity relative to distant fixed stars is an inertial frame.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <p>\n    Physicists classify inertia into three distinct physical manifestations:\n  </p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">1. Inertia of Rest:</b> The tendency of a body to remain at rest unless disturbed. <i>Examples:</i> When a bus abruptly starts moving forward, standing passengers jerk backward because their feet move forward with the floor while their upper bodies tend to stay stationary. When a tree branch is vigorously shaken, ripe fruits detach because the branches move while the fruits tend to remain at rest.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">2. Inertia of Motion:</b> The tendency of a moving body to maintain its velocity. <i>Examples:</i> When a speeding car slams on its brakes, passengers lurch forward because their bodies maintain forward velocity. An athlete runs a considerable distance before taking a long jump to gain inertia of motion.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">3. Inertia of Direction:</b> The tendency of a moving body to maintain its straight-line trajectory. <i>Examples:</i> When a vehicle takes a sharp turn around a curved bend, passengers are thrown outward along the tangent to the curve. Sparks flying off a grinding wheel travel strictly along the tangent to the rotating rim.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF8A65; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    3. Linear Momentum & Newton's Second Law of Motion\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    Common experience demonstrates that the destructive impact of a moving object depends simultaneously on both its mass and its velocity. A cricket ball thrown at 100 km/h hurts the hand severely, whereas a table-tennis ball moving at the same speed is caught effortlessly. Conversely, a tiny bullet weighing merely 10 grams fired from a rifle penetrates steel armor because of its immense velocity.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #FF8A65; color: #121212; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px;\">Linear Momentum (p)</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">The linear momentum of a body is defined as the total quantity of translational motion contained in it, measured mathematically as the product of its inertial mass and its instantaneous velocity vector.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFCC80; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">kg m s<sup>-1</sup> (or N s)</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFCC80; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    Mathematically: \u000bec{p} = m \u000bec{v}. Linear momentum is a vector quantity pointing in the exact spatial direction of the velocity vector \u000bec{v}.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #FF8A65; color: #121212; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px;\">Newton's Second Law of Motion</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">The time rate of change of linear momentum of an object is directly proportional to the applied unbalanced external force, and this change takes place along the direction in which the force acts.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFCC80; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">Newton (N)</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFCC80; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 15.5px;\">Mathematical Derivation of F = m a from Second Law</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.2); color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FF8A65;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Proportionality Statement</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">By Newton's Second Law: <b>F</b> &prop; <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>p</b></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> &rArr; <b>F</b> = k \\, <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>p</b></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>, where k is a constant of proportionality.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.2); color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FF8A65;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Substitute Momentum Vector</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Substitute <b>p</b> = m<b>v</b>:\n          <br><b>F</b> = k \\, <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>(m<b>v</b>) = k ( m \\, <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>v</b></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> + <b>v</b> \\, <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dm</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> ).</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.2); color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FF8A65;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Condition of Constant Mass</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">In non-relativistic classical mechanics, the mass m of a rigid particle is strictly constant (dm/dt = 0):\n          <br><b>F</b> = k \\, m \\, <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>v</b></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = k \\, m <b>a</b>.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.2); color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FF8A65;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Unit Calibration of Proportionality Constant</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">By SI definition, 1 Newton is the force which produces an acceleration of 1 m s<sup>-2</sup> in a mass of 1 kg.\n          <br>Substituting F = 1 N, m = 1 kg, a = 1 m s<sup>-2</sup> yields k = 1.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.2); color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FF8A65;\">5</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Final Master Vector Equation</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\"><div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n          <b>F</b> = m <b>a</b> = m \\, <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>v</b></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = m \\, <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<sup>2</sup><b>r</b></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt<sup>2</sup></span>\n  </span>\n        </div></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Component Form of Second Law:</b><br>\n    Since \u000bec{F} = m\u000bec{a} is a 3-dimensional vector relation, it resolves into three independent scalar Cartesian equations:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 10px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 12px 0; font-size: 15.5px; color: #FFCC80;\">\n    <b>F<sub>x</sub> = m a<sub>x</sub> = m <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d v_x</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>, quad F<sub>y</sub> = m a<sub>y</sub> = m <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d v_y</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>, quad F<sub>z</sub> = m a<sub>z</sub> = m <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d v_z</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span></b>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Proof that Newton's Second Law is the Real Law of Motion:</b><br>\n    Newton's Second Law is regarded as the master fundamental law because both the First Law and the Third Law are completely contained within it:\n  </p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">1. First Law Contained in Second Law:</b> From \u000bec{F} = m\u000bec{a}, if the net external force is zero (\u000bec{F} = \u000bec{0}), then since m \ne 0, we have \u000bec{a} = \u000bec{0}. Zero acceleration implies that velocity \u000bec{v} = \text{constant}. That is, an object at rest remains at rest (\u000bec{v} = \u000bec{0}), and an object in motion continues at constant velocity along a straight line. This is precisely Newton's First Law.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">2. Third Law Contained in Second Law:</b> Consider an isolated system of two interacting bodies A and B. In the absence of external forces (F_{\text{ext}} = 0), the total rate of change of momentum is zero: \frac{d\u000bec{p}_A}{dt} + \frac{d\u000bec{p}_B}{dt} = \u000bec{0} implies \u000bec{F}_{AB} + \u000bec{F}_{BA} = \u000bec{0} implies \u000bec{F}_{AB} = -\u000bec{F}_{BA}. This is precisely Newton's Third Law (Action = -Reaction).</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF8A65; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    4. Impulse & The Impulse-Momentum Theorem\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    In numerous real-world physical situations—such as a bat striking a cricket ball, a foot kicking a soccer ball, or an automobile collision—a very large force acts for an extremely short interval of time. Such forces are designated as <b>impulsive forces</b>. Because impulsive forces change violently during the fleeting millisecond contact duration, measuring the instantaneous force F(t) is practically impossible. Instead, we measure the total impact delivered, known as <b>Impulse</b>.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #FF8A65; color: #121212; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px;\">Impulse of a Force (J)</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Impulse is defined as the total effect of a force acting over a finite time interval, measured mathematically as the time integral of the force vector over that duration.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFCC80; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">N s (or kg m s<sup>-1</sup>)</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFCC80; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>]</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>The Impulse-Momentum Theorem:</b><br>\n    From Newton's Second Law: \u000bec{F} = \frac{d\u000bec{p}}{dt} implies d\u000bec{p} = \u000bec{F} , dt.<br>\n    Integrating both sides over the collision duration from t_1 to t_2:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 14px 0; font-size: 16px; color: #76FF03;\">\n    <b>\u000bec{J} = int_{t_1}<sup>t_2</sup> \u000bec{F} , dt = \u000bec{F}_{\text{avg}} Delta t = \u000bec{p}_2 - \u000bec{p}_1 = Delta\u000bec{p}</b>\n  </div>\n  <p>\n    <i>The impulse of an applied force equals the total change in linear momentum of the body.</i> On a Force-Time (F-t) graph, the impulse is geometrically represented by the <b>area under the curve</b> bounded by the time axis.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(14, 165, 233, 0.06); border-left: 4px solid #0EA5E9; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #38BDF8; font-size: 18px;\">💡</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #38BDF8; font-size: 15px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">REAL-WORLD INTUITION &bull; Why Catching a Fast Ball Requires Pulling Hands Back</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">To stop a moving cricket ball, its momentum must be brought to zero, so change in momentum &Delta;p is fixed. Since J = F_avg &times; &Delta;t = &Delta;p, increasing the stopping time &Delta;t by pulling hands backward drastically reduces the average force F_avg exerted on the palms, preventing painful bone injury.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 5 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF8A65; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    5. Newton's Third Law of Motion & Action-Reaction Pairs\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    Forces in nature never occur in isolation as single entities; they always arise as mutual interactions between pairs of bodies. If body A exerts a force on body B, body B simultaneously exerts an equal and opposite force on body A.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #FF8A65; color: #121212; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px;\">Newton's Third Law of Motion</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">To every action, there is always an equal and opposite reaction; or, the mutual actions of two bodies upon each other are always equal in magnitude and oppositely directed along the line joining them.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFCC80; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">—</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFCC80; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup>]</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    \u000bec{F}_{AB} = -\u000bec{F}_{BA} quad Longleftrightarrow quad |\u000bec{F}_{AB}| = |\u000bec{F}_{BA}|\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Essential Characteristics of Action-Reaction Pairs:</b>\n  </p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">1. They Act on Different Bodies:</b> Action acts on body B, while reaction acts on body A. Because they act on two entirely distinct bodies, <b>action and reaction can NEVER cancel each other out</b>.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">2. Simultaneous Occurrence:</b> Action and reaction arise at the exact same instant of time. There is no time delay between action and reaction; neither is the \"cause\" nor the other the \"effect\".</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">3. Same Physical Nature:</b> The action and reaction forces are always of the exact same physical origin (e.g. both gravitational, both normal contact, or both electrostatic).</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(239, 68, 68, 0.08); border: 1.5px solid #EF4444; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #F87171; font-size: 18px;\">⚠️</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #F87171; font-size: 14.5px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">EXAM TRAP & COMMON MISCONCEPTION: Normal Reaction vs Gravitational Weight</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FECACA; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;\">Students frequently state that Normal Reaction N and Weight mg form an action-reaction pair on a table. This is FALSE! Weight mg is the gravitational pull of the Earth on the book, whose true reaction is the book's gravitational pull on the Earth. Normal reaction N is the contact force of the table pushing up on the book, whose true reaction is the book pressing down on the table.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 6 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF8A65; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    6. Law of Conservation of Linear Momentum & Applications\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    The Principle of Conservation of Linear Momentum is one of the most profound and universal conservation laws in all of physics, holding true across quantum subatomic collisions as well as galactic astrophysics.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #FF8A65; color: #121212; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px;\">Law of Conservation of Linear Momentum</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">If no net external unbalanced force acts upon an isolated system of interacting particles, the total vector linear momentum of the system remains strictly constant over time.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFCC80; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">—</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFCC80; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">—</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Rigorous Mathematical Proof from Newton's Laws:</b><br>\n    Consider an isolated two-body system consisting of particles A and B. Let \u000bec{F}_{AB} be the force exerted on A by B, and \u000bec{F}_{BA} be the force exerted on B by A.<br>\n    By Newton's Second Law: \u000bec{F}_{AB} = \frac{d\u000bec{p}_A}{dt} and \u000bec{F}_{BA} = \frac{d\u000bec{p}_B}{dt}.<br>\n    By Newton's Third Law: \u000bec{F}_{AB} = -\u000bec{F}_{BA} implies \u000bec{F}_{AB} + \u000bec{F}_{BA} = \u000bec{0}.<br>\n    Therefore:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin: 12px 0;\">\n    <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>p</b>_A</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>p</b>_B</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>(\u000bec{p}_A + \u000bec{p}_B) = \u000bec{0} implies \u000bec{P}_{\text{total}} = \u000bec{p}_A + \u000bec{p}_B = \text{Constant Vector}\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Major Practical Applications:</b>\n  </p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">1. Recoil Velocity of a Gun:</b> Before firing, the gun and bullet are at rest, so total initial momentum is zero. When a bullet of mass m is discharged with muzzle velocity \u000bec{v}, the gun of mass M recoils backward with velocity \u000bec{V}_R to conserve momentum:\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #FFCC80; margin: 6px 0; font-size: 15px;\">\n        m\u000bec{v} + M\u000bec{V}_R = \u000bec{0} implies mathbf{\u000bec{V}_R = -<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">M</span>\n  </span> \u000bec{v}}\n      </div>\n      Because gun mass M gg m, recoil velocity V_R ll v.\n    </li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">2. Rocket Propulsion (Variable Mass System):</b> A rocket operates on the ejection of combustion gases at high speed u relative to the rocket nozzle. As gas of mass Delta m is expelled backward, the rocket recoils forward, experiencing instantaneous upward thrust:\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #FFCC80; margin: 6px 0; font-size: 15px;\">\n        mathbf{F_{\text{thrust}} = -u , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dm</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>} quad \text{and} quad mathbf{v(t) = v_0 + u lnleft(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_0</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m</span>\n  </span>\right) - gt}\n      </div>\n    </li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">3. Explosion of a Bomb at Rest:</b> A stationary bomb has zero momentum. When it detonates into three fragments of masses m_1, m_2, m_3 flying with velocities \u000bec{v}_1, \u000bec{v}_2, \u000bec{v}_3, their vector sum satisfies: m_1\u000bec{v}_1 + m_2\u000bec{v}_2 + m_3\u000bec{v}_3 = \u000bec{0}.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 7 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF8A65; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    7. Equilibrium of Concurrent Forces & Lami's Theorem\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    Forces whose lines of action pass through a single common point are termed <b>concurrent forces</b>. A body subjected to concurrent forces is said to be in <b>translational equilibrium</b> if its linear acceleration is identically zero (\u000bec{a} = \u000bec{0}).\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    <b>Condition for Equilibrium:</b> The vector sum of all concurrent forces acting on the particle must be zero:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin: 12px 0;\">\n    sum \u000bec{F} = \u000bec{F}_1 + \u000bec{F}_2 + dots + \u000bec{F}_n = \u000bec{0} quad Longleftrightarrow quad sum F_x = 0, ; sum F_y = 0, ; sum F_z = 0\n  </div>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #FF8A65; color: #121212; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px;\">Lami's Theorem</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">If three coplanar concurrent forces acting at a point keep a particle in static equilibrium, each force is directly proportional to the sine of the angle included between the other two forces.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFCC80; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">—</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFCC80; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">—</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    Mathematically, if forces \u000bec{F}_1, \u000bec{F}_2, \u000bec{F}_3 maintain equilibrium, and alpha, \beta, gamma are the angles opposite to \u000bec{F}_1, \u000bec{F}_2, \u000bec{F}_3 respectively:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 14px 0; font-size: 16.5px; color: #76FF03;\">\n    <b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">F_1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">sinalpha</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">F_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">sin\beta</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">F_3</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">singamma</span>\n  </span></b>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 8 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF8A65; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    8. Friction: Static, Kinetic, Rolling & Lubrication\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    Whenever a solid body moves or tends to move over the surface of another body, an opposing tangential force comes into play parallel to the contact interface. This resisting contact force is called <b>friction</b>.\n  </p>\n  <p>\n    <b>Physical Origin of Friction:</b><br>\n    Microscopic examination reveals that even the most mirror-polished surfaces possess irregular microscopic peaks (\"asperities\") and valleys. When two surfaces are placed in contact, they touch only at the high tips of these asperities. Consequently, the <i>actual contact area</i> is thousands of times smaller than the <i>apparent macroscopic area</i>. At these tiny contact tips, the local pressure is enormous, causing atomic bonds to weld together (cold welding). To slide one surface across another, these microscopic adhesive welds must be sheared apart.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Classification of Friction:</b>\n  </p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">1. Static Friction (f_s):</b> The opposing force that comes into play between two contact surfaces when there is an applied force tending to cause motion, but no relative motion has yet commenced. Static friction is a <b>self-adjusting force</b>: its magnitude exactly equals the applied external force (f_s = F_{\text{applied}}) up to a certain maximum threshold.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">2. Limiting Friction (f_L):</b> The maximum, ultimate value of static friction beyond which the body begins to slide.\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #FFCC80; margin: 8px 0; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n        mathbf{f_L = mu_s N} quad \text{where } mu_s \text{ is the coefficient of static friction.}\n      </div>\n    </li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">3. Kinetic (Sliding) Friction (f_k):</b> The opposing force that acts between two surfaces once relative sliding motion has actually commenced:\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #FFCC80; margin: 8px 0; font-size: 15.5px;\">\n        mathbf{f_k = mu_k N} quad \text{where } mu_k \text{ is the coefficient of kinetic friction.}\n      </div>\n      <b>Crucial Fact:</b> Because moving asperities do not get sufficient time to establish strong cold-welded junctions, <b>mu_k < mu_s</b> (kinetic friction is always strictly less than limiting friction).\n    </li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">4. Rolling Friction (f_r):</b> The resistance experienced when a spherical or cylindrical wheel rolls over a surface without slipping. Rolling friction is caused primarily by the local deformation of both the wheel and the supporting surface, creating a small uphill depression mound. Rolling friction is vastly smaller than sliding friction (f_r ll f_k ll f_s).</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <!-- COMPARISON TABLE: FRICTION TYPES -->\n  <div style=\"overflow-x: auto; margin: 20px 0;\">\n    <table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); font-size: 14px; border: 1px solid rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3);\">\n      <thead>\n        <tr style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.15); border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65;\">\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; color: #FF8A65; text-align: left;\">Feature</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; color: #FF8A65; text-align: left;\">Static Friction (f_s)</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; color: #FF8A65; text-align: left;\">Kinetic Friction (f_k)</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; color: #FF8A65; text-align: left;\">Rolling Friction (f_r)</th>\n        </tr>\n      </thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; font-weight: bold; color: #CBD5E1;\">Relative Motion</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Zero (impending motion only)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Active relative sliding</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Pure rolling without sliding</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; font-weight: bold; color: #CBD5E1;\">Magnitude</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Self-adjusting (0 le f_s le mu_s N)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Constant (f_k = mu_k N)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Minimal (f_r = mu_r N / R)</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; font-weight: bold; color: #CBD5E1;\">Relative Value</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Peak value f_L is highest</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Lower than limiting friction</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Smallest of all (~100 to 1000x smaller)</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; font-weight: bold; color: #CBD5E1;\">Primary Origin</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Interlocking & static cold welds</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Shearing of transient welds</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 9px; color: #E2E8F0;\">Surface deformation depression</td>\n        </tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Angle of Friction (&theta;) & Angle of Repose (&alpha;):</b>\n  </p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Angle of Friction (&theta;):</b> The angle that the resultant of limiting friction f_L and normal reaction N makes with the normal reaction vector:\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #FFCC80; margin: 6px 0; font-size: 15px;\">\n        \tan\theta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">f_L</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">N</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">mu_s N</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">N</span>\n  </span> = mathbf{mu_s} implies mathbf{\theta = \tan<sup>-1</sup>(mu_s)}\n      </div>\n    </li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Angle of Repose (&alpha;):</b> The minimum angle of inclination of a rough ramp at which a body placed upon it just begins to slide down under its own weight:\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #FFCC80; margin: 6px 0; font-size: 15px;\">\n        mgsinalpha = f_L = mu_s(mgcosalpha) implies \tanalpha = mathbf{mu_s} implies mathbf{alpha = \tan<sup>-1</sup>(mu_s)}\n      </div>\n    </li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">Fundamental Identity:</b> Since \tan\theta = mu_s and \tanalpha = mu_s, it follows that <b>\theta = alpha (Angle of Friction = Angle of Repose)</b>.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Why Pulling a Lawn Mower is Easier than Pushing It:</b><br>\n    When pushing a mower with force F at angle \theta below horizontal, the downward component Fsin\theta adds to weight, increasing normal reaction N = mg + Fsin\theta and raising friction to f_k = mu(mg + Fsin\theta). Conversely, when pulling at angle \theta above horizontal, the upward component Fsin\theta relieves normal reaction N = mg - Fsin\theta, reducing friction to f_k = mu(mg - Fsin\theta). Thus pulling encounters significantly less friction.\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Methods of Reducing Friction & Lubrication:</b>\n  </p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">1. Lubrication:</b> Applying liquid lubricants (engine oils), semi-solids (grease), or solid lubricants (graphite) forms a thin hydrodynamic film separating the metallic asperities, replacing solid-solid friction with much lower fluid-shear viscosity.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">2. Ball Bearings:</b> By placing hardened steel ball bearings between a rotating wheel axle and its housing, high-drag sliding friction is converted into microscopic rolling friction.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #FF8A65;\">3. Streamlining:</b> Shaping automobiles, airplanes, and high-speed bullet trains with aerodynamic teardrop profiles reduces fluid drag.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 9 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF8A65; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    9. Dynamics of Uniform Circular Motion & Banking of Roads\n  </h2>\n  <p>\n    In kinematics, we established that an object executing uniform circular motion undergoes continuous radial acceleration: a_c = v<sup>2</sup>/r. By Newton's Second Law, this inward acceleration requires a net inward radial force, termed the <b>Centripetal Force</b>.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.06); border: 1.5px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 10px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"background: #FF8A65; color: #121212; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">DEFINITION</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 16px;\">Centripetal Force (F_c)</b>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">The net inward radial force acting along the radius towards the center of a curved circular trajectory necessary to continuously alter the direction of an object's velocity vector.</div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 16px; margin-top: 10px; padding-top: 8px; border-top: 1px dashed rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3); font-size: 13.5px; color: #CBD5E1;\">\n      <span><b>SI Unit:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFCC80; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">Newton (N)</code></span>\n      <span><b>Dimensional Formula:</b> <code style=\"color: #FFCC80; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</code></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 14px 0; font-size: 16.5px; color: #76FF03;\">\n    <b>F_c = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">r</span>\n  </span> = m omega<sup>2</sup> r = 4pi<sup>2</sup> m \nu<sup>2</sup> r</b>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Case I: Vehicle on a Level Circular Road:</b><br>\n    When an automobile traverses an unbanked, flat circular road of radius r, the only horizontal force acting toward the center is the <b>static friction</b> between the tire rubber and the tarmac. The vertical forces are in balance: N = mg.<br>\n    For the vehicle to turn safely without skidding outward, the required centripetal force cannot exceed the maximum available static friction:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #FFCC80; margin: 10px 0; font-size: 16px;\">\n    <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">r</span>\n  </span> le f_L = mu_s N = mu_s m g implies v<sup>2</sup> le mu_s r g implies mathbf{v_{max} = sqrt{mu_s r g}}\n  </div>\n  <p>\n    <i>Notice that v_{max} is independent of vehicle mass m, but depends heavily on mu_s. On wet or icy roads where mu_s \to 0, level turning becomes impossible without skidding.</i>\n  </p>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Case II: Vehicle on a Banked Road (Elevated Outer Edge):</b><br>\n    To eliminate complete reliance on tire friction, curves on highways and race tracks are <b>banked</b>—meaning the outer edge of the curved roadway is elevated above the inner edge by an angle \theta.\n  </p>\n\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.3); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 14px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.2); padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 18px;\">📐</span>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-size: 15.5px;\">Derivation of Optimum & Maximum Safe Speeds on Banked Road</b>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.2); color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FF8A65;\">1</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Resolution of Normal Reaction</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">When the road is banked at angle &theta;, the normal reaction <b>N</b> tilts inward at angle &theta; to the vertical:\n          <br>&bull; Vertical component: N \\cos&theta; balances the downward weight and friction components.\n          <br>&bull; Horizontal component: N \\sin&theta; acts directly toward the center of curvature, providing centripetal force.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.2); color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FF8A65;\">2</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Optimum Speed (Zero Friction, mu = 0)</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">If we seek a speed v_0 where no friction is required at all (saving tire wear):\n          <br>N \\sin&theta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">r</span>\n  </span> and N \\cos&theta; = mg.\n          <br>Dividing these equations: \\tan&theta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">rg</span>\n  </span> &rArr; \\mathbf{v_0 = &radic;(rg\\tan&theta;)}.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.2); color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FF8A65;\">3</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Maximum Speed with Friction (Upper Safe Limit)</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">At maximum speed v_{\\max}, the vehicle tends to skid outward up the bank, generating limiting friction f_L = \\mu_s N directed downward along the banked incline.\n          <br>Resolving horizontally: N \\sin&theta; + f_L \\cos&theta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v_{\\max}<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">r</span>\n  </span> &rArr; N(\\sin&theta; + \\mu_s\\cos&theta;) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v_{\\max}<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">r</span>\n  </span>.\n          <br>Resolving vertically: N \\cos&theta; - f_L \\sin&theta; = mg &rArr; N(\\cos&theta; - \\mu_s\\sin&theta;) = mg.</div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; align-items: flex-start;\">\n      <span style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.2); color: #FF8A65; font-weight: bold; width: 24px; height: 24px; border-radius: 50%; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 12px; border: 1px solid #FF8A65;\">4</span>\n      <div style=\"flex: 1;\">\n        <div style=\"color: #FF8A65; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 2px;\">Final Master Banking Equation</div>\n        <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5;\">Dividing horizontal by vertical:\n          <br><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v_{\\max}<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">rg</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">N(\\sin&theta; + \\mu_s\\cos&theta;)</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">N(\\cos&theta; - \\mu_s\\sin&theta;)</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">\\mu_s + \\tan&theta;</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">1 - \\mu_s\\tan&theta;</span>\n  </span>.\n          <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; margin-top: 8px;\">\n            v_{\\max} = &radic;(rg ( <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">\\mu_s + \\tan&theta;</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">1 - \\mu_s\\tan&theta;</span>\n  </span> ))\n          </div></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n\n  <p>\n    <b>Case III: Bending of a Cyclist:</b><br>\n    A cyclist negotiating a sharp curve leans inward at an angle \theta with the vertical. The normal reaction from the ground tilts inward, so Nsin\theta provides centripetal force (Nsin\theta = mv<sup>2</sup>/r) while Ncos\theta balances weight (Ncos\theta = mg). Dividing gives:\n  </p>\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin: 12px 0;\">\n    \tan\theta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">rg</span>\n  </span> implies \theta = \tan<sup>-1</sup>left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">rg</span>\n  </span>\right)\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- MASTER REVISION FORMULA CHEAT SHEET -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-top: 35px; margin-bottom: 15px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">\n      ⚡ Master Revision Formula Cheat Sheet\n    </h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 0 0 16px 0; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Comprehensive Operational Equations &bull; Unit III: Laws of Motion (07 Marks)\n    </p>\n\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; font-size: 14px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Momentum & Second Law:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          \u000bec{p} = m \u000bec{v}<br>\n          \u000bec{F} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d\u000bec{p}</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = m \u000bec{a} = m , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<sup>2</sup>\u000bec{r}</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt<sup>2</sup></span>\n  </span><br>\n          \u000bec{F} = m <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d\u000bec{v}</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> + \u000bec{v} <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dm</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> quad \text{(Variable Mass)}\n        </span>\n      </div>\n\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Impulse & Third Law:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          \u000bec{J} = \u000bec{F}_{\text{avg}} Delta t = int \u000bec{F} dt = Delta\u000bec{p}<br>\n          \u000bec{J} = \text{Area under } F-t \text{ curve}<br>\n          \u000bec{F}_{AB} = -\u000bec{F}_{BA} quad \text{(Action-Reaction)}\n        </span>\n      </div>\n\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Momentum Conservation & Recoil:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          sum \u000bec{F}_{\text{ext}} = \u000bec{0} implies \u000bec{P}_{\text{total}} = \text{const}<br>\n          Recoil: V_R = -<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">M</span>\n  </span> v<br>\n          Rocket Thrust: F = -u , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dm</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span><br>\n          Rocket Velocity: v = u ln(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_0</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m</span>\n  </span>) - gt\n        </span>\n      </div>\n\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Equilibrium & Lami's Law:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          sum \u000bec{F} = \u000bec{0} Longleftrightarrow sum F_x = 0, sum F_y = 0<br>\n          <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">F_1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">sinalpha</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">F_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">sin\beta</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">F_3</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">singamma</span>\n  </span><br>\n          Atwood Accel: a = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 - m_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right) g<br>\n          Atwood Tension: T = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2 m_1 m_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right) g\n        </span>\n      </div>\n\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Laws of Friction:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          Limiting Friction: f_L = mu_s N<br>\n          Kinetic Friction: f_k = mu_k N quad (mu_k < mu_s)<br>\n          Angle of Friction: \tan\theta = mu_s<br>\n          Angle of Repose: \tanalpha = mu_s implies \theta = alpha<br>\n          Rough Incline Accel: a = g(sinalpha - mu_kcosalpha)\n        </span>\n      </div>\n\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Circular Motion & Banking:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          Centripetal Force: F_c = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">r</span>\n  </span> = momega<sup>2</sup> r<br>\n          Level Road Max Speed: v_{max} = sqrt{mu_s r g}<br>\n          Optimum Banking: \tan\theta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">rg</span>\n  </span> implies v_0 = sqrt{rg\tan\theta}<br>\n          Banked Max Speed: v_{max} = sqrt{rg left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">mu_s + \tan\theta</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">1 - mu_s\tan\theta</span>\n  </span>\right)}<br>\n          Cyclist Lean: \tan\theta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">rg</span>\n  </span>\n        </span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n</div>";

export const c11Phy3HtmlSolutions = "<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 16px;\">\n\n  <!-- TITLE BANNER -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #FF8A65; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px; text-align: center;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 22px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">\n      NCERT & Master Solutions Guide (100 Questions)\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 0; font-size: 15px; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Class 11 Physics Unit III: Laws of Motion (07 Marks) &bull; Official Syllabus Coverage\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION A: VSA QUESTIONS -->\n  <div style=\"margin-top: 25px; margin-bottom: 15px; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65; padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      Section A: Very Short Answer (VSA) Questions (1 Mark each) &bull; Q1 to Q40\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; margin: 4px 0 0 0; font-size: 14px;\">\n      Fundamental definitions, 1-line physical reasoning, SI units, dimensional formulas, and basic conceptual facts.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q1: Define force in classical mechanics. State its SI base unit and dimensional formula.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Force is an external push or pull that changes or tends to change the state of rest, uniform rectilinear motion, or shape and size of a body.<br><b>SI Unit:</b> Newton (N = kg m s<sup>-2</sup>) &bull; <b>Dimensional Formula:</b> [M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>].\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q2: What is meant by the 'Aristotelian Fallacy' regarding physical motion?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      The ancient Greek philosopher Aristotle asserted that an external force is continuously required to keep a body in uniform motion. This is a fallacy because in the absence of opposing resistive forces (like friction and air drag), no external force is needed to sustain constant velocity.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q3: State Newton's First Law of Motion. What two fundamental concepts does it establish?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      <b>Statement:</b> Every physical body continues in its state of rest or uniform motion in a straight line unless acted upon by an external unbalanced force.<br>It establishes: (1) <b>Inertia</b> of matter, and (2) <b>Qualitative definition of force</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q4: Define Inertia. What physical quantity serves as the direct quantitative measure of a body's inertia?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Inertia is the inherent property of a body by virtue of which it resists any alteration in its state of rest or uniform rectilinear motion.<br>The <b>inertial mass (m)</b> of the body is the direct quantitative measure of its inertia.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q5: Enumerate the three types of inertia with one classic practical example for each.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      1. <b>Inertia of Rest:</b> Dust particles fly out when a carpet is beaten with a stick.<br>2. <b>Inertia of Motion:</b> A passenger lurches forward when a speeding bus suddenly applies brakes.<br>3. <b>Inertia of Direction:</b> Sparks flying off a rotating grinding wheel travel tangentially.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q6: Why do passengers standing in a bus fall backward when the bus starts moving forward abruptly?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      When the bus starts abruptly, the lower part of the passengers' bodies in contact with the floor is accelerated forward by friction, while their upper bodies tend to remain stationary due to <b>inertia of rest</b>, causing them to fall backward.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q7: Why does an athlete run some distance before taking a long jump?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      By sprinting before jumping, the athlete acquires <b>inertia of motion</b>. This forward velocity adds to the propulsion force generated at takeoff, allowing the athlete to cover a greater horizontal flight distance.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q8: Why are mudguards placed over the wheels of moving vehicles?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Mud adhering to rotating tires flies off tangentially at every instant due to <b>inertia of direction</b>. Mudguards intercept these flying mud particles and prevent them from splashing over the vehicle or other motorists.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q9: What is an Inertial Frame of Reference? Give an example.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      An inertial frame of reference is a coordinate system in which Newton's laws of motion hold strictly true without introducing fictitious or pseudo-forces. A reference frame fixed to the Earth (approximated) or moving with constant velocity relative to deep space stars is an inertial frame.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q10: Is a rotating platform or turntable an inertial frame of reference? Justify.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      <b>No.</b> A rotating platform undergoes continuous centripetal acceleration (a = &omega;<sup>2</sup> r &ne; 0) directed toward its center of rotation. Any accelerating frame is a <b>non-inertial frame</b>, requiring pseudo-forces (centrifugal force) to analyze motion.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q11: Define Linear Momentum. Is it a scalar or a vector quantity? State its SI unit.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Linear momentum is defined as the total quantity of motion contained within a body, measured as the product of mass and velocity: <b>p = m v</b>.<br>It is a <b>vector quantity</b> pointing along the velocity vector. <b>SI Unit:</b> kg m s<sup>-1</sup> (or N s).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q12: State Newton's Second Law of Motion in its universal differential form.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      The time rate of change of linear momentum of an object is directly proportional to the applied net external force and takes place along the direction of the force: <b>F = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dp</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q13: Under what specific condition does the equation F = dp/dt reduce to F = m a?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Since p = mv, F = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>(mv) = m <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dv</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> + v <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dm</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>. The equation reduces strictly to <b>F = ma</b> when the inertial mass of the particle is <b>constant</b> (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dm</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = 0).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q14: Write the mathematical expression of Newton's Second Law for a variable mass system.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      For a variable mass system (such as an accelerating rocket burning propellant):<br><b>F = m <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dv</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> + v<sub>rel</sub> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dm</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span></b>, where v<sub>rel</sub> is the relative ejection velocity of mass dm/dt.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q15: Define the SI absolute unit of force: One Newton (1 N).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      <b>One Newton (1 N)</b> is defined as that magnitude of external unbalanced force which produces an acceleration of exactly 1 m s<sup>-2</sup> when acting on a body of mass 1 kg (1 N = 1 kg &times; 1 m s<sup>-2</sup>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q16: Define the CGS unit of force (1 dyne) and establish its mathematical relation with 1 Newton.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      <b>1 dyne</b> is that force which accelerates a mass of 1 gram by 1 cm s<sup>-2</sup>.<br>1 N = 1 kg &times; 1 m s<sup>-2</sup> = (10<sup>3</sup> g) &times; (10<sup>2</sup> cm s<sup>-2</sup>) = 10<sup>5</sup> g cm s<sup>-2</sup> = <b>10<sup>5</sup> dyne</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q17: Define 1 kilogram-force (1 kgf) and convert it into Newtons (g = 9.8 m/s²).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      <b>1 kgf</b> is the gravitational force exerted by Earth on a standard mass of 1 kilogram.<br>1 kgf = 1 kg &times; g = 1 kg &times; 9.8 m s<sup>-2</sup> = <b>9.8 N</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q18: Why is Newton's Second Law regarded as the fundamental law of motion?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Because both Newton's First Law and Newton's Third Law can be mathematically deduced from the Second Law. When F = 0, a = 0 (First Law); and in an isolated system with no external force, dp_total/dt = 0 leads to Action = -Reaction (Third Law).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q19: Define Impulse of a force. State its SI unit and dimensional formula.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Impulse is the total effect produced by a force acting over a finite duration, measured as the time integral of force: <b>J = &int; F dt = F<sub>avg</sub> &Delta;t</b>.<br><b>SI Unit:</b> N s (or kg m s<sup>-1</sup>) &bull; <b>Dimensional Formula:</b> [M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-1</sup>].\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q20: State the Impulse-Momentum Theorem.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      The impulse delivered to a particle by an applied force over any time interval is identically equal to the change in linear momentum of the particle: <b>J = &Delta;p = p<sub>2</sub> - p<sub>1</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q21: What physical quantity is represented by the geometric area under a Force-Time (F-t) graph?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      The area under the Force-Time (F-t) curve bounded by the time axis represents the <b>Impulse (J)</b> delivered by the force, which is equal to the <b>net change in linear momentum (&Delta;p)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q22: Why does a cricketer pull his hands backward while catching a high-speed cricket ball?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Pulling hands back lengthens the impact time interval &Delta;t. Since impulse &Delta;p = F<sub>avg</sub> &Delta;t is fixed, increasing &Delta;t substantially reduces the average impulsive force F<sub>avg</sub> exerted on the cricketer's palms, preventing injury.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q23: Why are automobiles manufactured with crumple zones and hydraulic shock absorbers?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Shock absorbers and crumple zones increase the duration &Delta;t over which collisions or road bumps occur, thereby dramatically minimizing the peak impulsive force transmitted to vehicle occupants.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q24: Why are fragile glassware and porcelain chinaware packed with straw, paper, or bubble wrap?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      The soft packing material deforms upon impact, increasing the deceleration time &Delta;t during drops or transport, thereby drastically reducing the impulsive force exerted on the brittle glassware.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q25: State Newton's Third Law of Motion in vector notation.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      To every action, there is always an equal and opposite reaction: <b>F<sub>AB</sub> = -F<sub>BA</sub></b>, where F<sub>AB</sub> is the force exerted on body A by body B, and F<sub>BA</sub> is the force exerted on body B by body A.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q26: Do action and reaction forces act on the same body or on different bodies?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Action and reaction forces <b>always act on two entirely different, interacting bodies</b>. Because they act on different bodies, they never balance or cancel each other out.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q27: A heavy horse pulls a light cart. If the force exerted by horse on cart equals the force exerted by cart on horse, how does the cart accelerate forward?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      The horse and cart accelerate because the horse pushes backward on the ground with force F, and the ground exerts an equal and opposite forward reaction force R on the horse's hooves. If this forward reaction exceeds wheel friction, the combined system accelerates.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q28: State the Principle of Conservation of Linear Momentum.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      In the absence of any net external unbalanced force (&Sigma; F<sub>ext</sub> = 0), the total vector linear momentum of an isolated system of interacting particles remains strictly constant over time.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q29: A rifle fires a bullet of mass m with muzzle speed v. If rifle mass is M, write its recoil speed.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      By conservation of linear momentum: 0 = m v + M V<sub>R</sub> &rArr; <b>V<sub>R</sub> = -<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">M</span>\n  </span> v</b>.<br>The negative sign confirms the rifle recoils in the direction opposite to bullet travel.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q30: On what fundamental physical law is the propulsion of rockets and jet engines based?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Rocket and jet propulsion is based on <b>Newton's Third Law of Motion</b> and the <b>Law of Conservation of Linear Momentum</b> in variable-mass reactive systems.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q31: Define Concurrent Forces.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Concurrent forces are forces whose lines of action pass through a single, common spatial point.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q32: What is the condition for translational equilibrium of a particle subjected to coplanar forces?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      The vector resultant of all forces acting on the particle must vanish: <b>&Sigma; F = 0</b>, which requires <b>&Sigma; F<sub>x</sub> = 0</b> and <b>&Sigma; F<sub>y</sub> = 0</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q33: State Lami's Theorem for three coplanar concurrent forces in equilibrium.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      If three concurrent forces F<sub>1</sub>, F<sub>2</sub>, F<sub>3</sub> keep a body in equilibrium: <b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">F<sub>1</sub></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">sin &alpha;</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">F<sub>2</sub></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">sin &beta;</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">F<sub>3</sub></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">sin &gamma;</span>\n  </span></b>, where &alpha;, &beta;, &gamma; are angles opposite to F<sub>1</sub>, F<sub>2</sub>, F<sub>3</sub>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q34: What is Static Friction? Why is it classified as a self-adjusting force?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Static friction is the opposing force between two contact surfaces that prevents impending relative motion. It is <b>self-adjusting</b> because its magnitude automatically matches the applied external force (f<sub>s</sub> = F<sub>applied</sub>) up to the limiting friction value.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q35: Define Limiting Friction (f_L) and write its formula in terms of normal reaction N.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Limiting friction is the maximum threshold value of static friction beyond which relative sliding commences: <b>f<sub>L</sub> = &mu;<sub>s</sub> N</b>, where &mu;<sub>s</sub> is the coefficient of static friction.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q36: Define Kinetic Friction (f_k). Why is the coefficient of kinetic friction less than static friction (&mu;_k < &mu;_s)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Kinetic friction is the opposing force between surfaces in actual relative sliding motion: <b>f<sub>k</sub> = &mu;<sub>k</sub> N</b>.<br>&mu;<sub>k</sub> < &mu;<sub>s</sub> because once sliding starts, contact asperities do not have enough time to form deep, interlocking cold-welded bonds.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q37: Define Angle of Friction (&theta;) and write its relation with the coefficient of static friction &mu;_s.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Angle of friction is the angle that the resultant of limiting friction f<sub>L</sub> and normal reaction N makes with the normal reaction: <b>tan &theta; = &mu;<sub>s</sub> &rArr; &theta; = tan<sup>-1</sup>(&mu;<sub>s</sub>)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q38: Define Angle of Repose (&alpha;). State its relationship with Angle of Friction (&theta;).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Angle of repose is the maximum inclination angle of a rough inclined plane at which a body placed on it remains at rest without sliding down under gravity: <b>tan &alpha; = &mu;<sub>s</sub></b>.<br>Therefore, <b>&alpha; = &theta; (Angle of Repose = Angle of Friction)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q39: Write the formula for the acceleration of a mass m sliding down a rough incline of angle &alpha; with kinetic friction &mu;_k.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      The net downward force is mg sin &alpha; - f<sub>k</sub> = mg sin &alpha; - &mu;<sub>k</sub> mg cos &alpha;.<br>Acceleration: <b>a = g (sin &alpha; - &mu;<sub>k</sub> cos &alpha;)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q40: State the formula for maximum safe speed of a vehicle rounding an unbanked, level circular road of radius r.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Standard Answer (1 Mark):</b>\n      Centripetal force is provided solely by static friction: mv<sup>2</sup>/r &le; &mu;<sub>s</sub> mg &rArr; <b>v<sub>max</sub> = &radic;(&mu;<sub>s</sub> r g)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION B: SA QUESTIONS -->\n  <div style=\"margin-top: 35px; margin-bottom: 15px; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65; padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      Section B: Short Answer (SA) Questions (2 & 3 Marks each) &bull; Q41 to Q80\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; margin: 4px 0 0 0; font-size: 14px;\">\n      Core board numericals, recoil calculations, friction problem-solving, Atwood machine analysis, and circular banking parameters.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q41: A constant retarding force of 50 N is applied to a body of mass 20 kg moving initially with a speed of 15 m/s. How long does the body take to come to rest, and what distance does it travel?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Given: m = 20 kg, u = 15 m/s, v = 0, F = -50 N.<br><b>(a) Acceleration:</b> a = F / m = -50 / 20 = <b>-2.5 m s<sup>-2</sup></b> (retardation = 2.5 m/s²).<br><b>(b) Time to rest:</b> Using v = u + at &rArr; 0 = 15 - 2.5 t &rArr; <b>t = 6 seconds</b>.<br><b>(c) Stopping distance:</b> Using v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> + 2as &rArr; 0 = (15)<sup>2</sup> + 2(-2.5)s &rArr; 5s = 225 &rArr; <b>s = 45 meters</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q42: A bullet of mass 0.04 kg moving with a speed of 90 m/s enters a heavy wooden block and is stopped after penetrating a distance of 60 cm. Estimate the average resistive force exerted by the block on the bullet.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Given: m = 0.04 kg, u = 90 m/s, v = 0, s = 60 cm = 0.60 m.<br>Using third equation of motion: v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> + 2as<br>0 = (90)<sup>2</sup> + 2 a (0.60) &rArr; 1.2 a = -8100 &rArr; <b>a = -6750 m s<sup>-2</sup></b>.<br><b>Average Resistive Force:</b> F = m a = 0.04 &times; (-6750) = <b>-270 N</b>.<br>The magnitude of the average resistive force is <b>270 Newtons</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q43: A force F = (6 t² - 4 t) N acts on a body of mass 2 kg initially at rest. Calculate: (a) Impulse imparted between t = 1 s and t = 3 s, (b) Velocity of the body at t = 3 s.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      <b>(a) Impulse:</b> J = &int;<sub>1</sub><sup>3</sup> F dt = &int;<sub>1</sub><sup>3</sup> (6 t<sup>2</sup> - 4 t) dt = [2 t<sup>3</sup> - 2 t<sup>2</sup>]<sub>1</sub><sup>3</sup>.<br>At t = 3: 2(27) - 2(9) = 54 - 18 = 36 N s.<br>At t = 1: 2(1) - 2(1) = 0 N s.<br><b>J = 36 - 0 = 36 N s (kg m s<sup>-1</sup>)</b>.<br><b>(b) Velocity at t = 3 s:</b> Since initial velocity at t = 0 is 0: v(t) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m</span>\n  </span> &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>3</sup> (6 t<sup>2</sup> - 4 t) dt = &frac12; [2(27) - 2(9)] = &frac12;(36) = <b>18 m s<sup>-1</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q44: Prove analytically that Newton's First Law of Motion is contained within Newton's Second Law.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      By Newton's Second Law: <b>F = m a</b>.<br>If no net external force acts on the body (F = 0):<br>0 = m a.<br>Since the mass of a physical body cannot be zero (m &ne; 0), the acceleration must vanish: <b>a = 0</b>.<br>Zero acceleration implies: <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dv</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = 0 &rArr; <b>v = constant</b>.<br>1. If the body was initially at rest (v = 0), it remains permanently at rest.<br>2. If the body was moving with velocity v, it continues moving with constant velocity in a straight line.<br>This is precisely the statement of Newton's First Law.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q45: Prove analytically that Newton's Third Law of Motion can be deduced from Newton's Second Law and the Principle of Conservation of Momentum.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Consider an isolated system consisting of two interacting bodies A and B with no external forces acting on them (F<sub>ext</sub> = 0).<br>Let F<sub>AB</sub> be force exerted on A by B, and F<sub>BA</sub> be force exerted on B by A.<br>Over a small collision time &Delta;t:<br>&bull; Change in momentum of A: &Delta;p<sub>A</sub> = F<sub>AB</sub> &Delta;t.<br>&bull; Change in momentum of B: &Delta;p<sub>B</sub> = F<sub>BA</sub> &Delta;t.<br>Total change in momentum: &Delta;p<sub>total</sub> = &Delta;p<sub>A</sub> + &Delta;p<sub>B</sub> = (F<sub>AB</sub> + F<sub>BA</sub>) &Delta;t.<br>Since F<sub>ext</sub> = 0, by conservation of linear momentum: &Delta;p<sub>total</sub> = 0.<br>(F<sub>AB</sub> + F<sub>BA</sub>) &Delta;t = 0 &rArr; <b>F<sub>AB</sub> = -F<sub>BA</sub></b>.<br>Thus, Action = -Reaction, which is Newton's Third Law.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q46: A batsman deflects a cricket ball of mass 0.15 kg by an angle of 45° without changing its initial speed of 54 km/h. What is the impulse imparted to the ball?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Mass m = 0.15 kg, speed u = 54 km/h = 54 &times; (5/18) = <b>15 m s<sup>-1</sup></b>.<br>Let incoming trajectory be along x-axis. Deflection angle = 45&deg;.<br>&bull; Initial velocity: v<sub>i</sub> = 15 &icirc;.<br>&bull; Final velocity: v<sub>f</sub> = 15 cos 45&deg; &icirc; + 15 sin 45&deg; &jcirc;.<br>Change in velocity: &Delta;v = 15(cos 45&deg; - 1)&icirc; + 15 sin 45&deg; &jcirc;.<br>Magnitude: |&Delta;v| = 2 u sin(&theta;/2) = 2(15) sin(22.5&deg;) = 30 &times; 0.3827 = 11.48 m/s.<br><b>Impulse imparted:</b> J = m |&Delta;v| = 0.15 &times; 11.48 &approx; <b>1.72 N s (or kg m s<sup>-1</sup>)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q47: Explain why a person jumping from a height onto a hard concrete floor sustains severe injuries, whereas jumping onto loose sand or an inflated cushion causes no harm.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      When a jumper strikes a concrete floor, the rigid surface does not yield, bringing momentum to zero over an extremely short time interval &Delta;t<sub>1</sub> (~0.01 s).<br>Since Impulse &Delta;p = F<sub>avg</sub> &Delta;t is constant, F<sub>avg</sub> = &Delta;p / &Delta;t.<br>A very small &Delta;t<sub>1</sub> produces an enormous peak impact force that shatters bones.<br>On loose sand or a cushion, the surface yields and compresses, prolonging the impact time &Delta;t<sub>2</sub> by 10 to 50 times. Consequently, F<sub>avg</sub> is reduced to a harmless, survivable level.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q48: A machine gun fires 240 bullets per minute with a muzzle speed of 500 m/s. If the mass of each bullet is 10 grams, calculate the average force required to hold the gun stationary.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Mass of each bullet: m = 10 g = 0.01 kg.<br>Number of bullets per second: n = 240 / 60 = <b>4 bullets s<sup>-1</sup></b>.<br>Muzzle speed: v = 500 m s<sup>-1</sup>.<br>Momentum carried away per bullet: p = m v = 0.01 &times; 500 = 5 kg m s<sup>-1</sup>.<br>Rate of change of momentum of bullets: <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">&Delta;p</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">&Delta;t</span>\n  </span> = n &times; (m v) = 4 &times; 5 = <b>20 N</b>.<br>By Newton's Third Law, the bullets exert a backward recoil force of 20 N on the gun.<br>Therefore, the operator must exert a forward force of <b>20 Newtons</b> to hold the gun in place.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q49: A bomb of mass 30 kg flying with a velocity of 48 m/s explodes into two fragments of masses 18 kg and 12 kg. If the 18 kg fragment is brought to instantaneous rest, find the velocity of the 12 kg fragment.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Total mass M = 30 kg, initial velocity V = 48 m/s.<br>Initial momentum: P<sub>initial</sub> = M V = 30 &times; 48 = <b>1440 kg m s<sup>-1</sup></b>.<br>After explosion: m<sub>1</sub> = 18 kg with v<sub>1</sub> = 0; m<sub>2</sub> = 12 kg with velocity v<sub>2</sub>.<br>Final momentum: P<sub>final</sub> = m<sub>1</sub> v<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub> v<sub>2</sub> = (18 &times; 0) + 12 v<sub>2</sub> = 12 v<sub>2</sub>.<br>By conservation of linear momentum: P<sub>initial</sub> = P<sub>final</sub> &rArr; 1440 = 12 v<sub>2</sub>.<br><b>v<sub>2</sub> = 1440 / 12 = 120 m s<sup>-1</sup></b> in the original direction of flight.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q50: A rocket of initial mass 6000 kg ejects fuel exhaust gases at a constant speed of 1000 m/s relative to the rocket at a rate of 16 kg/s. Calculate: (a) Upward thrust on the rocket, (b) Initial acceleration of the rocket (g = 9.8 m/s²).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Given: m<sub>0</sub> = 6000 kg, u = 1000 m/s, <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dm</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = 16 kg/s.<br><b>(a) Upward Thrust:</b> F<sub>thrust</sub> = u <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dm</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = 1000 &times; 16 = <b>16,000 N (16 kN)</b>.<br><b>(b) Initial Acceleration:</b> Gravitational weight W = m<sub>0</sub> g = 6000 &times; 9.8 = 58,800 N.<br>Net force F<sub>net</sub> = F<sub>thrust</sub> - m<sub>0</sub> g.<br>Here F<sub>thrust</sub> (16,000 N) < Weight (58,800 N), so the rocket would not lift off vertically unless thrust exceeds weight. If launched horizontally in deep space: a = F<sub>thrust</sub> / m<sub>0</sub> = 16000 / 6000 = <b>2.67 m s<sup>-2</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q51: State and derive the condition for equilibrium of three concurrent coplanar forces using the vector triangle method.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Let three concurrent forces F<sub>1</sub>, F<sub>2</sub>, F<sub>3</sub> act at a common point O.<br>For translational equilibrium: <b>F<sub>1</sub> + F<sub>2</sub> + F<sub>3</sub> = 0 &rArr; F<sub>1</sub> + F<sub>2</sub> = -F<sub>3</sub></b>.<br>This states that the resultant of any two forces must be equal in magnitude, collinear, and opposite in direction to the third force.<br><b>Triangle Rule:</b> If three concurrent forces can be represented in magnitude and direction by the three sides of a triangle taken in the same cyclic order, their vector sum is identically zero and the particle is in equilibrium.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q52: A mass of 10 kg is suspended by a rope from a ceiling. A horizontal force F pulls the rope such that it makes an angle of 30° with the vertical. Calculate: (a) Tension in the rope, (b) Magnitude of the horizontal force F (g = 9.8 m/s²).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Let T be the tension in the rope inclined at 30&deg; to the vertical. Weight W = m g = 10 &times; 9.8 = <b>98 N</b>.<br>Resolving forces on the joint:<br>&bull; Vertical: T cos 30&deg; = W = 98 N &rArr; T(&radic;3 / 2) = 98 &rArr; <b>T = 196 / &radic;3 &approx; 113.16 N</b>.<br>&bull; Horizontal: F = T sin 30&deg; = 113.16 &times; 0.5 = <b>56.58 N</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q53: Two masses m_1 and m_2 (m_1 > m_2) are connected to the ends of a light, inextensible string passing over a frictionless, massless pulley (Atwood's Machine). Derive expressions for the acceleration of the system and the tension in the string.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Let m<sub>1</sub> accelerate downward with acceleration a, and m<sub>2</sub> accelerate upward with acceleration a. Tension throughout the string is T.<br>&bull; For mass m<sub>1</sub>: m<sub>1</sub> g - T = m<sub>1</sub> a &nbsp;&hellip;(1)<br>&bull; For mass m<sub>2</sub>: T - m<sub>2</sub> g = m<sub>2</sub> a &nbsp;&hellip;(2)<br><b>1. Acceleration:</b> Adding (1) and (2):<br>(m<sub>1</sub> - m<sub>2</sub>)g = (m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub>)a &rArr; <b>a = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m<sub>1</sub> - m<sub>2</sub></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub></span>\n  </span> g</b>.<br><b>2. Tension:</b> Dividing (1) by (2):<br><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m<sub>1</sub> g - T</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">T - m<sub>2</sub> g</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m<sub>1</sub></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m<sub>2</sub></span>\n  </span> &rArr; <b>T = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2 m<sub>1</sub> m<sub>2</sub></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub></span>\n  </span> g</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q54: In an Atwood machine, two masses of 8 kg and 12 kg are attached to the string. Taking g = 9.8 m/s², find the acceleration of the masses and the tension in the string.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Given: m<sub>1</sub> = 12 kg, m<sub>2</sub> = 8 kg, g = 9.8 m/s².<br><b>Acceleration:</b> a = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m<sub>1</sub> - m<sub>2</sub></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub></span>\n  </span> g = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">12 - 8</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">12 + 8</span>\n  </span> &times; 9.8 = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">4</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">20</span>\n  </span> &times; 9.8 = 0.2 &times; 9.8 = <b>1.96 m s<sup>-2</sup></b>.<br><b>Tension:</b> T = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2 m<sub>1</sub> m<sub>2</sub></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m<sub>1</sub> + m<sub>2</sub></span>\n  </span> g = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2 &times; 12 &times; 8</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">20</span>\n  </span> &times; 9.8 = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">192</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">20</span>\n  </span> &times; 9.8 = 9.6 &times; 9.8 = <b>94.08 N</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q55: Distinguish between static friction, limiting friction, and kinetic friction. Sketch the characteristic curve showing how frictional force varies with applied external force.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      1. <b>Static Friction (f_s):</b> Opposes impending motion when body is at rest; self-adjusting from 0 to f<sub>L</sub>.<br>2. <b>Limiting Friction (f_L):</b> Maximum threshold of static friction before sliding begins (f<sub>L</sub> = &mu;<sub>s</sub> N).<br>3. <b>Kinetic Friction (f_k):</b> Opposes actual relative sliding motion; constant and less than f<sub>L</sub> (f<sub>k</sub> = &mu;<sub>k</sub> N).<br><b>Curve Profile:</b> A linear ramp of slope 1 (f<sub>s</sub> = F<sub>applied</sub>) up to a peak sharp crest at f<sub>L</sub>, followed by a slight drop to a flat horizontal plateau representing f<sub>k</sub>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q56: State the four empirical Laws of Limiting Friction.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      1. The direction of limiting friction is always tangential to the contact surfaces and opposes the direction of impending motion.<br>2. The magnitude of limiting friction f<sub>L</sub> is directly proportional to the normal reaction N between the surfaces: <b>f<sub>L</sub> &prop; N &rArr; f<sub>L</sub> = &mu;<sub>s</sub> N</b>.<br>3. Limiting friction is independent of the apparent macroscopic surface area of contact, provided normal reaction remains constant.<br>4. Limiting friction depends entirely on the material nature and degree of microscopic roughness of the contacting surfaces.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q57: Define Angle of Friction (&theta;) and Angle of Repose (&alpha;). Prove analytically that Angle of Friction equals Angle of Repose.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      <b>Angle of Friction (&theta;):</b> The angle made by the resultant of limiting friction f<sub>L</sub> and normal reaction N with the normal reaction: tan &theta; = f<sub>L</sub> / N = (&mu;<sub>s</sub> N) / N = <b>&mu;<sub>s</sub></b>.<br><b>Angle of Repose (&alpha;):</b> The angle of inclination of a rough incline at which a body placed on it just begins to slide down under gravity:<br>Along the incline: mg sin &alpha; = f<sub>L</sub> = &mu;<sub>s</sub> N.<br>Perpendicular to incline: N = mg cos &alpha;.<br>Dividing: tan &alpha; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">mg sin &alpha;</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">mg cos &alpha;</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">&mu;<sub>s</sub> N</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">N</span>\n  </span> = <b>&mu;<sub>s</sub></b>.<br>Since tan &theta; = &mu;<sub>s</sub> and tan &alpha; = &mu;<sub>s</sub>, it strictly follows that: <b>&theta; = &alpha;</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q58: Explain why it is easier to pull a lawn mower than to push it, using vector resolution of forces.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Let force F be applied at angle &theta; to the horizontal.<br><b>1. Pushing:</b> The force is directed downward into the ground. Normal reaction is N = mg + F sin &theta;. Kinetic friction is <b>f<sub>push</sub> = &mu;(mg + F sin &theta;)</b>.<br><b>2. Pulling:</b> The force is directed upward away from the ground. Normal reaction is N = mg - F sin &theta;. Kinetic friction is <b>f<sub>pull</sub> = &mu;(mg - F sin &theta;)</b>.<br>Because N is reduced during pulling, frictional resistance is significantly smaller, making <b>pulling much easier than pushing</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q59: A block of mass 4 kg rests on a rough horizontal surface with &mu;_s = 0.4 and &mu;_k = 0.3 (g = 9.8 m/s²). Calculate the frictional force and acceleration when applied horizontal force is: (a) 12 N, (b) 20 N.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Normal reaction: N = mg = 4 &times; 9.8 = <b>39.2 N</b>.<br>Limiting friction: f<sub>L</sub> = &mu;<sub>s</sub> N = 0.4 &times; 39.2 = <b>15.68 N</b>.<br>Kinetic friction: f<sub>k</sub> = &mu;<sub>k</sub> N = 0.3 &times; 39.2 = <b>11.76 N</b>.<br><b>(a) Applied Force F = 12 N:</b> Since F < f<sub>L</sub> (12 < 15.68), the block remains at rest. By self-adjusting static friction: <b>f<sub>s</sub> = 12 N</b>, and acceleration <b>a = 0 m s<sup>-2</sup></b>.<br><b>(b) Applied Force F = 20 N:</b> Since F > f<sub>L</sub> (20 > 15.68), the block accelerates. Friction drops to kinetic friction: <b>f<sub>k</sub> = 11.76 N</b>.<br>Net force = 20 - 11.76 = 8.24 N &rArr; a = 8.24 / 4 = <b>2.06 m s<sup>-2</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q60: A wooden block slides down an inclined plane of inclination 30° with a uniform constant speed. What is the coefficient of kinetic friction between the block and the plane?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      When a body moves down an incline at constant speed, its acceleration is zero (a = 0).<br>Net force along incline = mg sin &theta; - f<sub>k</sub> = 0 &rArr; mg sin &theta; = &mu;<sub>k</sub> N.<br>Since N = mg cos &theta;, we have: mg sin &theta; = &mu;<sub>k</sub> mg cos &theta;.<br>&mu;<sub>k</sub> = tan &theta; = tan 30&deg; = 1 / &radic;3 &approx; <b>0.577</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q61: A block of mass 2 kg is placed on a rough inclined plane making an angle of 45° with the horizontal. If the coefficient of kinetic friction is 0.2, calculate the acceleration down the incline (g = 9.8 m/s²).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Given: m = 2 kg, &theta; = 45&deg;, &mu;<sub>k</sub> = 0.2, g = 9.8 m/s².<br>Acceleration down incline: a = g (sin &theta; - &mu;<sub>k</sub> cos &theta;).<br>sin 45&deg; = cos 45&deg; = 1 / &radic;2 &approx; 0.7071.<br>a = 9.8 &times; (0.7071 - 0.2 &times; 0.7071) = 9.8 &times; 0.7071 &times; (1 - 0.2) = 9.8 &times; 0.7071 &times; 0.8 = <b>5.54 m s<sup>-2</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q62: Explain why Rolling Friction is vastly smaller than Sliding Friction. How do ball bearings exploit this principle?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      <b>Cause of Rolling Friction:</b> When a wheel rolls, it deforms the contact surface, creating a small depression ahead of it. The wheel must climb over this tiny mound, but there is zero relative tangential rubbing between contact surfaces.<br>Sliding friction involves shearing thousands of cold-welded microscopic asperities, whereas rolling involves momentary compressive contact without tearing welds.<br><b>Ball Bearings:</b> Hardened steel balls placed between an axle and outer casing convert sliding contact into pure rolling contact, reducing friction by up to 90%.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q63: Enumerate three practical methods of reducing friction and two engineering situations where friction is deliberately increased.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      <b>Three Methods of Reducing Friction:</b><br>1. <b>Lubrication:</b> Engine oils and grease form fluid films separating metallic asperities.<br>2. <b>Ball Bearings:</b> Converting sliding friction into rolling friction.<br>3. <b>Streamlining:</b> Aerodynamic contouring of aircraft and cars to reduce fluid drag.<br><b>Two Situations where Friction is Increased:</b><br>1. <b>Tire Treads:</b> Grooves on vehicle tires maintain grip and expel water to prevent hydroplaning.<br>2. <b>Brake Pads:</b> High-friction composite pads clamp onto brake discs to stop vehicles.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q64: Derive the expression for the maximum safe speed with which a vehicle can round an unbanked, level circular curve of radius r without skidding.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      On a flat circular road of radius r, vertical forces balance: N = mg.<br>The sole horizontal force directed toward the center is static friction f<sub>s</sub>, which provides the centripetal acceleration:<br>f<sub>s</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">r</span>\n  </span>.<br>To prevent skidding, required friction cannot exceed limiting friction f<sub>L</sub> = &mu;<sub>s</sub> N = &mu;<sub>s</sub> mg:<br><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">r</span>\n  </span> &le; &mu;<sub>s</sub> mg &rArr; v<sup>2</sup> &le; &mu;<sub>s</sub> r g.<br><b>Maximum Safe Speed: v<sub>max</sub> = &radic;(&mu;<sub>s</sub> r g)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q65: A car rounds an unbanked horizontal curve of radius 50 m on a road with &mu;_s = 0.2. What is the maximum speed in km/h to prevent skidding? (g = 9.8 m/s²)</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Given: r = 50 m, &mu;<sub>s</sub> = 0.2, g = 9.8 m/s².<br>v<sub>max</sub> = &radic;(&mu;<sub>s</sub> r g) = &radic;(0.2 &times; 50 &times; 9.8) = &radic;(10 &times; 9.8) = &radic;98 &approx; <b>9.90 m s<sup>-1</sup></b>.<br>In km/h: 9.90 &times; (18/5) = <b>35.64 km/h</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q66: Why are roads banked at curves? Derive the expression for the Optimum Speed of a vehicle on a banked road without relying on friction.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      <b>Need for Banking:</b> On flat curves, centripetal force relies entirely on tire friction, which varies unpredictably with rain, ice, or tire wear. Banking the outer edge creates a horizontal component of normal reaction that directs inward, reducing reliance on friction.<br><b>Optimum Speed:</b> In the absence of friction (&mu; = 0):<br>&bull; N cos &theta; = mg (vertical balance)<br>&bull; N sin &theta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">r</span>\n  </span> (centripetal force)<br>Dividing: tan &theta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">rg</span>\n  </span> &rArr; <b>v<sub>0</sub> = &radic;(r g tan &theta;)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q67: A circular racetrack of radius 300 m is banked at an angle of 15°. If &mu;_s = 0.2 and g = 9.8 m/s², calculate: (a) Optimum speed, (b) Maximum permissible safe speed.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      r = 300 m, &theta; = 15&deg; (tan 15&deg; &approx; 0.2679), &mu;<sub>s</sub> = 0.2, g = 9.8 m/s².<br><b>(a) Optimum Speed:</b> v<sub>0</sub> = &radic;(r g tan &theta;) = &radic;(300 &times; 9.8 &times; 0.2679) = &radic;787.6 = <b>28.06 m s<sup>-1</sup> (101 km/h)</b>.<br><b>(b) Maximum Safe Speed:</b><br>v<sub>max</sub> = &radic;[ r g (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">&mu;<sub>s</sub> + tan &theta;</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">1 - &mu;<sub>s</sub> tan &theta;</span>\n  </span>) ]<br><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">0.2 + 0.2679</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">1 - 0.2 &times; 0.2679</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">0.4679</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">0.9464</span>\n  </span> &approx; 0.4944.<br>v<sub>max</sub> = &radic;(300 &times; 9.8 &times; 0.4944) = &radic;1453.5 = <b>38.12 m s<sup>-1</sup> (137.2 km/h)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q68: Explain why a cyclist bends inward while taking a turn. Derive the expression for the angle of lean with the vertical.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      A cyclist negotiating a curve must generate an inward centripetal force. If upright, normal reaction from the ground is vertical and cannot provide centripetal force, causing outward tipping. By leaning inward at angle &theta; with the vertical, the ground reaction R tilts inward:<br>&bull; R sin &theta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">r</span>\n  </span> (centripetal force)<br>&bull; R cos &theta; = mg (vertical weight support)<br>Dividing gives: <b>tan &theta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">rg</span>\n  </span> &rArr; &theta; = tan<sup>-1</sup>(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">rg</span>\n  </span>)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q69: A cyclist speeding at 18 km/h negotiates a circular turn of radius 3 m without slipping. What is the minimum angle of inclination with the vertical? (g = 9.8 m/s²)</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Speed: v = 18 km/h = 18 &times; (5/18) = <b>5 m s<sup>-1</sup></b>. Radius: r = 3 m, g = 9.8 m/s².<br>tan &theta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">rg</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">5<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">3 &times; 9.8</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">25</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">29.4</span>\n  </span> &approx; 0.8503.<br><b>&theta; = tan<sup>-1</sup>(0.8503) &approx; 40.37°</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q70: A mass of 0.5 kg attached to the end of a string 1.2 m long is whirled in a horizontal circle at 3 revolutions per second. Calculate the tension in the string.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Given: m = 0.5 kg, r = 1.2 m, &nu; = 3 rev/s.<br>Angular speed: &omega; = 2&pi; &nu; = 2 &times; 3.1416 &times; 3 = <b>18.85 rad s<sup>-1</sup></b>.<br>Centripetal force is provided solely by string tension T:<br>T = m &omega;<sup>2</sup> r = 0.5 &times; (18.85)<sup>2</sup> &times; 1.2 = 0.6 &times; 355.3 = <b>213.2 Newtons</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q71: A person of mass m stands on a weighing machine in an elevator. Derive the apparent weight recorded by the machine when the elevator: (a) Accelerates upward with a, (b) Accelerates downward with a, (c) Falls freely.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      The weighing scale records the normal contact reaction N.<br><b>(a) Accelerating Upward with a:</b> Net force is upward: N - mg = ma &rArr; <b>N = m (g + a)</b> (Apparent weight increases).<br><b>(b) Accelerating Downward with a:</b> Net force is downward: mg - N = ma &rArr; <b>N = m (g - a)</b> (Apparent weight decreases).<br><b>(c) Free Fall (a = g):</b> N = m (g - g) = <b>0</b> (Complete state of weightlessness).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q72: A 70 kg person stands inside an elevator. Calculate the apparent weight when the elevator: (a) Ascends with acceleration 2 m/s², (b) Descends with acceleration 2 m/s², (c) Descends at constant speed 5 m/s (g = 9.8 m/s²).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Mass m = 70 kg, g = 9.8 m/s².<br><b>(a) Ascending at a = 2 m/s²:</b> N = m(g + a) = 70(9.8 + 2) = 70 &times; 11.8 = <b>826 N</b>.<br><b>(b) Descending at a = 2 m/s²:</b> N = m(g - a) = 70(9.8 - 2) = 70 &times; 7.8 = <b>546 N</b>.<br><b>(c) Constant Speed (a = 0):</b> N = m g = 70 &times; 9.8 = <b>686 N</b> (true weight).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q73: Explain why a horse cannot pull a cart and accelerate in deep outer space or on frictionless ice.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      To move forward, the horse must push backward against the ground with its hooves. By Newton's Third Law, the ground reacts with an equal and opposite forward reaction force R. On frictionless ice or in empty space, no horizontal ground reaction can be developed (R = 0), making forward acceleration impossible.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q74: Why does a heavy rifle kick back with noticeably less severity than a light pistol firing an identical bullet?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      By conservation of linear momentum, recoil velocity is V<sub>R</sub> = -(m/M)v. Because the mass M of a heavy rifle is much greater than that of a lightweight pistol, its recoil velocity V<sub>R</sub> is much smaller. Consequently, the recoil kinetic energy delivered to the shooter's shoulder (&frac12; M V<sub>R</sub><sup>2</sup> = p<sup>2</sup> / 2M) is substantially less.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q75: A 1000 kg car moving at 72 km/h crashes into a concrete wall and comes to a halt in 0.05 seconds. Calculate the average force exerted on the vehicle and the total impulse delivered.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Mass m = 1000 kg, initial speed u = 72 km/h = <b>20 m s<sup>-1</sup></b>, final v = 0, &Delta;t = 0.05 s.<br><b>Impulse:</b> J = &Delta;p = m(0 - u) = 1000 &times; (-20) = <b>-20,000 N s</b> (Magnitude = 20,000 N s).<br><b>Average Force:</b> F<sub>avg</sub> = J / &Delta;t = -20000 / 0.05 = <b>-400,000 N (-400 kN)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q76: Explain how conservation of momentum applies to the recoil of an artillery cannon, and why an artillery crew stands clear of the gun tube.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      Before discharge, total momentum is zero. When the heavy shell is propelled forward with immense velocity, the massive cannon tube recoils backward with velocity V<sub>R</sub> = -(m/M)v to keep total momentum zero. Modern artillery uses hydraulic recoil buffers to absorb this backward stroke, but any crew standing behind the recoiling breech would be crushed.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q77: Three concurrent coplanar forces act on a body: F_1 = (2 î + 3 ĵ) N, F_2 = (-5 î + ĵ) N, and F_3. If the body is in equilibrium, find the magnitude and direction of F_3.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      For translational equilibrium: F<sub>1</sub> + F<sub>2</sub> + F<sub>3</sub> = 0.<br>F<sub>3</sub> = -(F<sub>1</sub> + F<sub>2</sub>) = -[(2 - 5)&icirc; + (3 + 1)&jcirc;] = -[-3 &icirc; + 4 &jcirc;] = <b>3 &icirc; - 4 &jcirc; N</b>.<br><b>Magnitude:</b> |F<sub>3</sub>| = &radic;[3<sup>2</sup> + (-4)<sup>2</sup>] = &radic;[9 + 16] = <b>5 Newtons</b>.<br><b>Direction:</b> &theta; = tan<sup>-1</sup>(-4/3) &approx; -53.13&deg; (in fourth quadrant).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q78: What is lubrication? Distinguish between thick-film (hydrodynamic) lubrication and thin-film (boundary) lubrication.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      <b>Lubrication</b> is the introduction of a substance between moving surfaces to reduce friction and wear.<br>&bull; <b>Thick-Film Lubrication:</b> A continuous thick viscous fluid layer completely separates solid surfaces so asperities never touch; friction depends solely on lubricant fluid viscosity.<br>&bull; <b>Thin-Film (Boundary) Lubrication:</b> A microscopic molecular layer adsorbs onto metal surfaces; some asperity contact occurs, but cold-welding is substantially prevented.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q79: A car of mass 1200 kg travels around an unbanked circular curve of radius 80 m at 54 km/h. Find the centripetal force required and the minimum coefficient of friction needed.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      m = 1200 kg, r = 80 m, v = 54 km/h = <b>15 m s<sup>-1</sup></b>, g = 9.8 m/s².<br><b>Centripetal Force:</b> F<sub>c</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">r</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">1200 &times; (15)<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">80</span>\n  </span> = 15 &times; 225 = <b>3375 Newtons</b>.<br><b>Minimum Friction Coefficient:</b> &mu;<sub>s</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">rg</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">225</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">80 &times; 9.8</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">225</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">784</span>\n  </span> &approx; <b>0.287</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q80: Explain why railway tracks are banked (superelevation), and write the formula for the height h by which the outer rail is elevated above the inner rail.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Proof:</b>\n      When a train negotiates a curve, centripetal force is required. If rails are level, the wheel flanges press hard against the outer rail, causing severe rail wear and danger of derailment. By elevating the outer rail by height h across track gauge width G, the normal reaction tilts inward:<br>tan &theta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">h</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">G</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">rg</span>\n  </span> &rArr; <b>h = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">G v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">rg</span>\n  </span></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION C: LA QUESTIONS -->\n  <div style=\"margin-top: 35px; margin-bottom: 15px; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF8A65; padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      Section C: Long Answer (LA) Questions (5 Marks each) &bull; Q81 to Q100\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; margin: 4px 0 0 0; font-size: 14px;\">\n      Comprehensive multi-step board derivations (Second Law as fundamental law, impulse-momentum theorem, rocket propulsion, banking speeds, pulling vs pushing, and master synthesis).\n    </p>\n  </div>\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q81: State Newton's Second Law of Motion. Derive the master vector relation F = m a for a body of constant mass, explain how the absolute SI unit of force (1 Newton) is calibrated, and write the law in Cartesian component form.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Statement of Newton's Second Law:</b><br>\n      The time rate of change of linear momentum of an object is directly proportional to the net applied unbalanced external force, and this rate of change takes place along the spatial direction in which the force acts.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Mathematical Derivation (\u000bec{F} = m\u000bec{a}):</b><br>\n      Let a particle of mass m move with instantaneous velocity \u000bec{v}.<br>\n      Its linear momentum is: \u000bec{p} = m \u000bec{v}.<br>\n      By Newton's Second Law: \u000bec{F} propto <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>p</b></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> implies \u000bec{F} = k , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>p</b></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>, where k is a constant of proportionality.<br>\n      Substituting \u000bec{p} = m\u000bec{v} and applying the product rule of calculus:<br>\n      \u000bec{F} = k , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>(m\u000bec{v}) = k left( m , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>v</b></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> + \u000bec{v} , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dm</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> \right).<br>\n      For a rigid body moving at speeds far below the speed of light, mass is strictly constant (dm/dt = 0):<br>\n      \u000bec{F} = k , m , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>v</b></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = k , m \u000bec{a}, where \u000bec{a} = d\u000bec{v}/dt is instantaneous acceleration.<br><br>\n      <b>3. Unit Calibration of k:</b><br>\n      The SI absolute unit of force, the <b>Newton (N)</b>, is defined such that a force of 1\text{ N} acting on a mass of 1\text{ kg} produces an acceleration of 1\text{ m s}<sup>-2</sup>.<br>\n      Substituting F = 1\text{ N}, m = 1\text{ kg}, and a = 1\text{ m s}<sup>-2</sup> into F = k m a yields:<br>\n      1 = k(1)(1) implies mathbf{k = 1}.<br>\n      Hence, the fundamental vector equation is:\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        \u000bec{F} = m \u000bec{a} = m , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>v</b></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = m , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<sup>2</sup><b>r</b></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt<sup>2</sup></span>\n  </span>\n      </div>\n      <b>4. Cartesian Component Form:</b><br>\n      Since force and acceleration are 3D vectors: \u000bec{F} = F_xhat{i} + F_yhat{j} + F_zhat{k} and \u000bec{a} = a_xhat{i} + a_yhat{j} + a_zhat{k}.<br>\n      Equating components along each orthogonal axis:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #FFCC80; font-size: 15px;\">\n        F_x = m a_x = m , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d v_x</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>, quad F_y = m a_y = m , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d v_y</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>, quad F_z = m a_z = m , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d v_z</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q82: Prove analytically that Newton's Second Law of Motion is the 'Real Law of Motion' by demonstrating that: (a) Newton's First Law is completely contained within the Second Law, and (b) Newton's Third Law can be deduced from the Second Law.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Conceptual Thesis:</b><br>\n      Newton's Second Law is termed the fundamental or 'real' law of motion because both the First Law (Law of Inertia) and the Third Law (Law of Action-Reaction) are logical, mathematical consequences contained within it.<br><br>\n      <b>(a) Deducing Newton's First Law from the Second Law:</b><br>\n      By Newton's Second Law: \u000bec{F} = m\u000bec{a}.<br>\n      If the net external force acting on the body is zero (\u000bec{F} = \u000bec{0}):<br>\n      \u000bec{0} = m\u000bec{a}.<br>\n      Since the inertial mass of a material object cannot be zero (m \ne 0), we must have:<br>\n      \u000bec{a} = \u000bec{0} implies <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>v</b></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = \u000bec{0} implies \u000bec{v} = \text{Constant Vector}.<br>\n      &bull; If the body was initially at rest (\u000bec{v} = \u000bec{0}), it continues to remain at rest indefinitely.<br>\n      &bull; If the body was in motion with velocity \u000bec{v}, it continues moving at constant speed along the exact same straight line.<br>\n      This is precisely the statement of Newton's First Law. Hence, <b>the First Law is contained within the Second Law</b>.<br><br>\n      <b>(b) Deducing Newton's Third Law from the Second Law:</b><br>\n      Consider an isolated system consisting of two interacting bodies A and B upon which no external forces act (\u000bec{F}_{\text{ext}} = \u000bec{0}).<br>\n      Let \u000bec{F}_{AB} be the force exerted on A by B, and \u000bec{F}_{BA} be the force exerted on B by A.<br>\n      By Newton's Second Law, the rate of change of momentum of each body is:<br>\n      \u000bec{F}_{AB} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>p</b>_A</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> quad \text{and} quad \u000bec{F}_{BA} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>p</b>_B</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      Adding both equations:<br>\n      \u000bec{F}_{AB} + \u000bec{F}_{BA} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>p</b>_A</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>p</b>_B</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>(\u000bec{p}_A + \u000bec{p}_B) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>P</b>_{total}</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      Since no external forces act on the system, the total linear momentum must be conserved (d\u000bec{P}_{total}/dt = \u000bec{0}):<br>\n      \u000bec{F}_{AB} + \u000bec{F}_{BA} = \u000bec{0} implies mathbf{\u000bec{F}_{AB} = -\u000bec{F}_{BA}}.<br>\n      This proves that Action is equal and opposite to Reaction, which is Newton's Third Law. Hence, <b>both laws are contained within the Second Law</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q83: Define Impulse. State and derive the Impulse-Momentum Theorem from Newton's Second Law. Explain three practical everyday applications where impulsive forces are modified to prevent injury or damage.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Definition of Impulse (\u000bec{J}):</b><br>\n      Impulse is the measure of the total effect produced by a force acting over a finite time interval, defined mathematically as the time integral of the force vector:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        \u000bec{J} = int_{t_1}<sup>t_2</sup> \u000bec{F} , dt = \u000bec{F}_{avg} Delta t\n      </div>\n      <b>2. Derivation of the Impulse-Momentum Theorem:</b><br>\n      By Newton's Second Law: \u000bec{F} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>p</b></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      Rearranging: d\u000bec{p} = \u000bec{F} , dt.<br>\n      Integrating both sides from initial state (t_1, \u000bec{p}_1) to final state (t_2, \u000bec{p}_2):<br>\n      int_{\u000bec{p}_1}<sup>\u000bec{p</sup>_2} d\u000bec{p} = int_{t_1}<sup>t_2</sup> \u000bec{F} , dt.<br>\n      [\u000bec{p}]_{\u000bec{p}_1}<sup>\u000bec{p</sup>_2} = \u000bec{J} implies mathbf{\u000bec{J} = \u000bec{p}_2 - \u000bec{p}_1 = Delta\u000bec{p}}.<br>\n      <i>The impulse delivered by an applied force equals the net change in linear momentum produced by it.</i><br><br>\n      <b>3. Three Everyday Applications:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>1. Catching a Cricket Ball:</b> A fielder catches a fast-moving ball by pulling his hands backward. Because Delta\u000bec{p} is fixed, increasing the impact duration Delta t drastically reduces the average force \u000bec{F}_{avg} = Delta\u000bec{p}/Delta t on his hands, preventing broken bones.<br>\n      &bull; <b>2. Automobile Airbags & Crumple Zones:</b> During a frontal collision, the crumple zone collapses and airbags inflate, lengthening deceleration time Delta t by a factor of 10 to 20, thereby reducing the impulsive force exerted on the driver's chest and head below lethal thresholds.<br>\n      &bull; <b>3. Packing Brittle Chinaware in Bubble Wrap:</b> Fragile porcelain is enveloped in air-filled bubble wrap so that any shock or drop deforms the wrap, prolonging collision time Delta t and cushioning against sharp impulsive force spikes.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q84: State the Principle of Conservation of Linear Momentum. Derive it rigorously for an isolated system of two colliding particles using: (a) Newton's Third Law, (b) Newton's Second Law.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Statement:</b><br>\n      The total linear momentum of an isolated system (a system subjected to zero net external unbalanced force, sum \u000bec{F}_{ext} = \u000bec{0}) remains strictly conserved in magnitude and direction, regardless of internal interactions or collisions among the particles.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Derivation using Newton's Third Law:</b><br>\n      Consider two particles A and B of masses m_1 and m_2 moving with initial velocities \u000bec{u}_1 and \u000bec{u}_2.<br>\n      They collide for a short duration Delta t and emerge with final velocities \u000bec{v}_1 and \u000bec{v}_2.<br>\n      During contact:<br>\n      &bull; Force on A due to B is \u000bec{F}_{AB} = m_1 left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\"><b>v</b>_1 - <b>u</b>_1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">&Delta;t</span>\n  </span>\right).<br>\n      &bull; Force on B due to A is \u000bec{F}_{BA} = m_2 left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\"><b>v</b>_2 - <b>u</b>_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">&Delta;t</span>\n  </span>\right).<br>\n      By Newton's Third Law: \u000bec{F}_{AB} = -\u000bec{F}_{BA}.<br>\n      m_1 left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\"><b>v</b>_1 - <b>u</b>_1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">&Delta;t</span>\n  </span>\right) = -m_2 left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\"><b>v</b>_2 - <b>u</b>_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">&Delta;t</span>\n  </span>\right).<br>\n      Multiplying by Delta t:<br>\n      m_1\u000bec{v}_1 - m_1\u000bec{u}_1 = -(m_2\u000bec{v}_2 - m_2\u000bec{u}_2) = -m_2\u000bec{v}_2 + m_2\u000bec{u}_2.<br>\n      Rearranging terms:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        m_1\u000bec{u}_1 + m_2\u000bec{u}_2 = m_1\u000bec{v}_1 + m_2\u000bec{v}_2 implies \u000bec{P}_{initial} = \u000bec{P}_{final}\n      </div>\n      <b>3. Derivation using Newton's Second Law:</b><br>\n      For the total system: \u000bec{F}_{ext} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>P</b>_{total}</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      For an isolated system, \u000bec{F}_{ext} = \u000bec{0} implies <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">d<b>P</b>_{total}</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = \u000bec{0}.<br>\n      Integrating yields: mathbf{\u000bec{P}_{total} = sum m_i \u000bec{v}_i = Constant Vector}.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q85: Derive an analytical formula for the Recoil Velocity of a gun of mass M firing a bullet of mass m with muzzle speed v. Prove that the kinetic energy of the bullet is substantially greater than the recoil kinetic energy of the gun.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Recoil Velocity Derivation:</b><br>\n      Let the gun of mass M and bullet of mass m be initially at rest before firing.<br>\n      Initial momentum of system: \u000bec{P}_{initial} = \u000bec{0}.<br>\n      Upon detonation of the cartridge, the bullet leaves the barrel with muzzle velocity \u000bec{v}.<br>\n      Let the gun recoil backward with velocity \u000bec{V}_R.<br>\n      Since the chemical explosion forces are internal to the gun-bullet system, external force is zero (\u000bec{F}_{ext} = \u000bec{0}).<br>\n      By the Law of Conservation of Linear Momentum:<br>\n      \u000bec{P}_{initial} = \u000bec{P}_{final} implies \u000bec{0} = m\u000bec{v} + M\u000bec{V}_R.<br>\n      Solving for recoil velocity:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        \u000bec{V}_R = -<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">M</span>\n  </span> \u000bec{v} quad implies quad V_R = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">M</span>\n  </span> v\n      </div>\n      The negative sign indicates that the gun kicks back in the direction opposite to bullet flight.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Kinetic Energy Comparison:</b><br>\n      Kinetic energy of bullet: K_b = \frac{1}{2} m v<sup>2</sup> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">p<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2m</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      Kinetic energy of recoiling gun: K_g = \frac{1}{2} M V_R<sup>2</sup> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">p<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">2M</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      Since both have identical momentum magnitudes (p_b = p_g = p):<br>\n      <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">K_b</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">K_g</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">p<sup>2</sup> / (2m)</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">p<sup>2</sup> / (2M)</span>\n  </span> = mathbf{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">M</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m</span>\n  </span>}.<br>\n      Because gun mass is vastly greater than bullet mass (M gg m), it follows that:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        K_b gg K_g\n      </div>\n      Almost all the explosive chemical energy is transferred as kinetic energy to the bullet, while only a small fraction appears as recoil energy in the gun.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q86: Formulate the theory of Rocket Propulsion as a variable mass system: Derive expressions for: (a) Upward thrust on the rocket, (b) Instantaneous acceleration at time t, (c) Burnt-out velocity of the rocket.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Conceptual Physical Model:</b><br>\n      A rocket accelerates by ejecting combustion gases at high speed u relative to the nozzle. Since propellant is burned continuously, the mass of the rocket decreases with time, making it a classic <b>variable-mass system</b>.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Derivations:</b><br>\n      Let initial mass of rocket with fuel at t = 0 be m_0.<br>\n      At time t, mass is m and upward velocity is v.<br>\n      In time interval dt, mass dm of gas is expelled downward with speed u relative to the rocket, so its velocity relative to Earth is (v - u).<br>\n      The rocket's mass becomes (m - dm) and its velocity increases to (v + dv).<br><br>\n      By conservation of linear momentum:<br>\n      m v = (m - dm)(v + dv) + dm(v - u)<br>\n      m v = m v + m , dv - v , dm - dm , dv + v , dm - u , dm.<br>\n      Neglecting the second-order differential product dm , dv approx 0:<br>\n      0 = m , dv - u , dm implies m , dv = u , dm.<br>\n      Dividing by dt:<br>\n      m , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dv</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> = u , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dm</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>.<br><br>\n      <b>(a) Upward Thrust (F_{thrust}):</b><br>\n      Since mass is decreasing, rate of fuel consumption is -dm/dt:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        F_{thrust} = -u , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dm</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span>\n      </div>\n      <b>(b) Instantaneous Acceleration under Gravity (a):</b><br>\n      Taking downward gravitational pull mg into account:<br>\n      F_{net} = F_{thrust} - mg implies m a = -u , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dm</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> - mg.<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        a(t) = -<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">u</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m</span>\n  </span> , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dm</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">dt</span>\n  </span> - g\n      </div>\n      <b>(c) Burnt-Out Velocity (v_b):</b><br>\n      From dv = -u \frac{dm}{m} - g , dt, integrating from t = 0 (v = 0, m = m_0) to burn-out (v = v_b, m = m_b):<br>\n      int_0<sup>v_b</sup> dv = -u int_{m_0}<sup>m_b</sup> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">dm</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m</span>\n  </span> - g int<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup> dt<br>\n      v_b = -u [ln m]_{m_0}<sup>m_b</sup> - gt = -u (ln m_b - ln m_0) - gt<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        v_b = u lnleft(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_0</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_b</span>\n  </span>\right) - gt\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q87: State Lami's Theorem for three coplanar concurrent forces in equilibrium. Provide a complete analytical proof using the sine rule of vector triangles.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Statement of Lami's Theorem:</b><br>\n      If three coplanar concurrent forces acting at a point keep a particle in static equilibrium, each force is directly proportional to the sine of the angle included between the remaining two forces:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">F_1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">\\sin\\alpha</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">F_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">\\sin\\beta</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">F_3</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">\\sin&gamma;</span>\n  </span>\n      </div>\n      where alpha is the angle between \u000bec{F}_2 and \u000bec{F}_3, \beta is between \u000bec{F}_3 and \u000bec{F}_1, and gamma is between \u000bec{F}_1 and \u000bec{F}_2.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Analytical Proof:</b><br>\n      Let three forces \u000bec{F}_1, \u000bec{F}_2, \u000bec{F}_3 act concurrently at point O in equilibrium:<br>\n      \u000bec{F}_1 + \u000bec{F}_2 + \u000bec{F}_3 = \u000bec{0} implies \u000bec{F}_1 + \u000bec{F}_2 = -\u000bec{F}_3.<br>\n      By the triangle law of vector addition, these three forces can be represented both in magnitude and direction by the three sides of a closed triangle ABC taken in cyclic order:<br>\n      \u000bec{AB} = \u000bec{F}_1, \u000bec{BC} = \u000bec{F}_2, \u000bec{CA} = \u000bec{F}_3.<br><br>\n      The interior angles of triangle ABC are related to the angles between the vectors by exterior angle relations:<br>\n      &bull; Interior angle at A = 180<sup>c</sup>irc - \beta<br>\n      &bull; Interior angle at B = 180<sup>c</sup>irc - gamma<br>\n      &bull; Interior angle at C = 180<sup>c</sup>irc - alpha<br><br>\n      Applying the standard trigonometric Sine Rule to Delta ABC:<br>\n      <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">BC</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">\\sin(\\angle A)</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">CA</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">\\sin(\\angle B)</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">AB</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">\\sin(\\angle C)</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      Substitute the force magnitudes and angles:<br>\n      <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">F_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">\\sin(180\\circ - \\beta)</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">F_3</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">\\sin(180\\circ - &gamma;)</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">F_1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">\\sin(180\\circ - \\alpha)</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      Since sin(180<sup>c</sup>irc - \theta) = sin\theta, this yields:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">F_1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">\\sin\\alpha</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">F_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">\\sin\\beta</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">F_3</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">\\sin&gamma;</span>\n  </span>\n      </div>\n      This completes the analytical proof.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q88: In an Atwood Machine, two unequal masses m_1 and m_2 (with m_1 > m_2) are suspended over a light, frictionless, fixed pulley by a light inextensible string. Derive: (a) Acceleration of the system, (b) Tension in the string, (c) Downward thrust on the pulley support.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Free-Body Diagram (FBD) Equations:</b><br>\n      Because the string is inextensible, both masses move with identical acceleration magnitude a. Mass m_1 moves downward while mass m_2 moves upward.<br>\n      Because the pulley is light and frictionless, tension T is uniform throughout the string.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Equation of motion for m_1 (downward):</b><br>\n      m_1 g - T = m_1 a quad implies quad T = m_1(g - a) quad ldots(1)<br>\n      &bull; <b>Equation of motion for m_2 (upward):</b><br>\n      T - m_2 g = m_2 a quad implies quad T = m_2(g + a) quad ldots(2)<br><br>\n      <b>(a) Acceleration of the System (a):</b><br>\n      Equating the expressions for T from (1) and (2):<br>\n      m_1 g - m_1 a = m_2 g + m_2 a implies (m_1 - m_2)g = (m_1 + m_2)a.<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        a = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 - m_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right) g\n      </div>\n      <b>(b) Tension in the String (T):</b><br>\n      Substitute a into equation (2):<br>\n      T = m_2(g + a) = m_2left[ g + left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 - m_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right)g \right] = m_2 g left[ <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">(m_1 + m_2) + (m_1 - m_2)</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span> \right]<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        T = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">2 m_1 m_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right) g\n      </div>\n      <b>(c) Force on the Pulley Support (F_P):</b><br>\n      Two string segments pull straight down on the pulley, each with tension T. Neglecting pulley mass:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        F_P = 2 T = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">4 m_1 m_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right) g\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q89: Two bodies of masses m_1 and m_2 connected by a light string lie on a smooth horizontal surface. A horizontal pulling force F is applied to m_1. Derive expressions for: (a) Acceleration of the system, (b) Tension in the connecting string. Extend the result to three connected bodies.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Two Connected Bodies:</b><br>\n      Let horizontal force F pull mass m_1 to the right, and let mass m_2 be connected to m_1 by a taut string with tension T. Surface is smooth (mu = 0).<br>\n      Both masses accelerate together with acceleration a.<br>\n      &bull; <b>For mass m_1:</b> F - T = m_1 a quad ldots(1)<br>\n      &bull; <b>For mass m_2:</b> T = m_2 a quad ldots(2)<br><br>\n      <b>(a) Acceleration:</b><br>\n      Adding (1) and (2):<br>\n      F = (m_1 + m_2)a implies mathbf{a = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">F</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>}.<br><br>\n      <b>(b) Tension:</b><br>\n      Substitute a into (2):<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        T = m_2 a = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_2</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right) F\n      </div>\n      <b>2. Extension to Three Connected Bodies (m_1, m_2, m_3):</b><br>\n      Let m_1 be pulled by F, connected to m_2 by string with tension T_1, and m_2 connected to m_3 by string with tension T_2.<br>\n      Total mass = m_1 + m_2 + m_3.<br>\n      System acceleration: mathbf{a = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">F</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2 + m_3</span>\n  </span>}.<br>\n      &bull; Tension T_1 pulls (m_2 + m_3): mathbf{T_1 = (m_2 + m_3) a = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_2 + m_3</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2 + m_3</span>\n  </span>\right) F}.<br>\n      &bull; Tension T_2 pulls m_3 alone: mathbf{T_2 = m_3 a = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_3</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2 + m_3</span>\n  </span>\right) F}.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q90: A block of mass m_1 on a rough horizontal table (&mu;_k) is connected by a light string over a frictionless pulley to a hanging mass m_2. Derive expressions for: (a) Common acceleration of the blocks, (b) Tension in the string, (c) Minimum mass m_2 required to initiate motion from rest (&mu;_s).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Free Body Equations of Motion:</b><br>\n      Let hanging mass m_2 descend with acceleration a, pulling horizontal mass m_1 across the table.<br>\n      Normal reaction on m_1: N = m_1 g.<br>\n      Kinetic friction on m_1: f_k = mu_k N = mu_k m_1 g.<br>\n      &bull; <b>For mass m_1:</b> T - f_k = m_1 a implies T - mu_k m_1 g = m_1 a quad ldots(1)<br>\n      &bull; <b>For mass m_2:</b> m_2 g - T = m_2 a quad ldots(2)<br><br>\n      <b>(a) Acceleration:</b><br>\n      Adding (1) and (2):<br>\n      m_2 g - mu_k m_1 g = (m_1 + m_2)a<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        a = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_2 - mu_k m_1</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right) g\n      </div>\n      <b>(b) Tension in String:</b><br>\n      Multiply (1) by m_2 and (2) by m_1, then subtract:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        T = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m_1 m_2 (1 + mu_k)</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">m_1 + m_2</span>\n  </span>\right) g\n      </div>\n      <b>(c) Condition to Initiate Motion:</b><br>\n      To start motion from rest, the downward gravitational pull of m_2 must exceed the maximum limiting static friction on m_1 (f_L = mu_s m_1 g):<br>\n      m_2 g > mu_s m_1 g implies mathbf{m_2 > mu_s m_1}.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q91: Explain the microscopic origin of friction using both the classical mechanical roughness model and the modern molecular adhesion (cold-welding) theory. Why does mirror-polishing beyond a certain limit increase friction rather than decreasing it?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Classical Mechanical Roughness Theory:</b><br>\n      Macroscopically smooth surfaces possess microscopic hills (asperities) and valleys. The classical view attributed friction to the mechanical interlocking of these jagged asperities. Moving one surface requires lifting or shearing these interlocking peaks.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Modern Molecular Adhesion (Cold-Welding) Theory:</b><br>\n      When two surfaces are placed in contact, they touch only at the tiny summit tips of asperities. Consequently, the <b>true contact area (A_{true})</b> is only approx 10<sup>-4</sup> of the apparent surface area (A_{apparent}).<br>\n      Under the massive localized pressure (P = N / A_{true}), the atoms at the contact tips come within interatomic bond distances (sim 10<sup>-10</sup>\text{ m}), forming strong chemical/metallic bonds known as <b>cold-welded micro-junctions</b>.<br>\n      Friction is primarily the tangential shearing force required to rupture these atomic welds: f = A_{true} \times \tau_{shear}.<br><br>\n      <b>3. The Paradox of Excessive Polishing:</b><br>\n      Moderate polishing removes large asperities, decreasing mechanical roughness and reducing friction.<br>\n      However, if surfaces are <b>excessively mirror-polished (super-finished)</b>, the surfaces become so flat that the actual contact area A_{true} expands dramatically. Millions more surface atoms are brought into direct atomic contact, creating massive intermolecular adhesion. As a result, the force required to break these bonds increases, and <b>friction increases sharply</b> (seizing or cold galling).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q92: Define Angle of Friction (&theta;) and Angle of Repose (&alpha;). Prove analytically that &theta; = &alpha;, and derive the expression for acceleration of a body sliding down an inclined plane when inclination angle exceeds the angle of repose.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Angle of Friction (\theta):</b><br>\n      The angle that the resultant of limiting friction \u000bec{f}_L and normal reaction \u000bec{N} makes with the normal reaction:<br>\n      \tan\theta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">f_L</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">N</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">mu_s N</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">N</span>\n  </span> = mu_s implies mathbf{\theta = \tan<sup>-1</sup>(mu_s)}.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Angle of Repose (alpha):</b><br>\n      The maximum angle of inclination of a rough incline at which a body placed upon it just rests in equilibrium without sliding down under gravity.<br>\n      At impending slip:<br>\n      &bull; Along incline: mg sinalpha = f_L<br>\n      &bull; Normal to incline: N = mg cosalpha<br>\n      Dividing: \tanalpha = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">f_L</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">N</span>\n  </span> = mu_s implies mathbf{alpha = \tan<sup>-1</sup>(mu_s)}.<br><br>\n      <b>3. Equivalence:</b><br>\n      Since \tan\theta = mu_s and \tanalpha = mu_s, it strictly follows that: <b>\theta = alpha (Angle of Friction = Angle of Repose)</b>.<br><br>\n      <b>4. Acceleration down Incline (alpha > \theta):</b><br>\n      When inclination angle alpha exceeds the angle of repose, the downward gravitational component mgsinalpha exceeds limiting friction and sliding begins.<br>\n      Normal reaction: N = mg cosalpha.<br>\n      Kinetic friction: f_k = mu_k N = mu_k mg cosalpha.<br>\n      Net downward accelerating force along the incline:<br>\n      F_{net} = mg sinalpha - f_k = mg sinalpha - mu_k mg cosalpha.<br>\n      Applying F_{net} = ma:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        a = g(sinalpha - mu_k cosalpha)\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q93: Prove analytically, using complete resolution of forces, why it is noticeably easier to PULL a heavy lawn roller than to PUSH it across the ground.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Pushing the Lawn Roller:</b><br>\n      Let a compressive pushing force F be applied at an angle \theta below the horizontal.<br>\n      Resolving force F into rectangular components:<br>\n      &bull; Horizontal forward component: F cos\theta<br>\n      &bull; Vertical downward component: F sin\theta<br>\n      The downward component acts in the same direction as gravity (mg).<br>\n      Vertical equilibrium equation:<br>\n      N_1 - mg - F sin\theta = 0 implies mathbf{N_1 = mg + F sin\theta}.<br>\n      Kinetic friction opposing motion is:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 6px 0; color: #FF8A65; font-size: 15px;\">\n        f_{push} = mu_k N_1 = mu_k (mg + F sin\theta)\n      </div>\n      Net forward accelerating force: F_{net, push} = F cos\theta - mu_k(mg + F sin\theta).<br><br>\n      <b>2. Pulling the Lawn Roller:</b><br>\n      Let an identical pulling force F be applied at an angle \theta above the horizontal.<br>\n      Resolving force F into rectangular components:<br>\n      &bull; Horizontal forward component: F cos\theta<br>\n      &bull; Vertical upward component: F sin\theta<br>\n      The upward component relieves the ground pressure.<br>\n      Vertical equilibrium equation:<br>\n      N_2 + F sin\theta - mg = 0 implies mathbf{N_2 = mg - F sin\theta}.<br>\n      Kinetic friction opposing motion is:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 6px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 15px;\">\n        f_{pull} = mu_k N_2 = mu_k (mg - F sin\theta)\n      </div>\n      Net forward accelerating force: F_{net, pull} = F cos\theta - mu_k(mg - F sin\theta).<br><br>\n      <b>3. Comparison:</b><br>\n      Since N_2 < N_1, we have f_{pull} < f_{push}. The difference in frictional drag is:<br>\n      Delta f = f_{push} - f_{pull} = 2 mu_k F sin\theta > 0.<br>\n      <b>Hence, pulling encounters vastly less frictional drag, making pulling significantly easier than pushing.</b>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q94: Derive the expression for the minimum force required to drag a body of mass m along a rough horizontal plane. Prove that the pulling force is an absolute minimum when applied at an angle equal to the angle of friction.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Force Resolution Setup:</b><br>\n      Let a pulling force F be inclined at an angle \theta with the horizontal.<br>\n      Resolving F:<br>\n      &bull; Horizontal component: F cos\theta<br>\n      &bull; Vertical upward component: F sin\theta<br>\n      Vertical equilibrium: N + Fsin\theta = mg implies N = mg - Fsin\theta.<br>\n      Frictional force opposing impending motion: f = mu N = mu(mg - Fsin\theta).<br><br>\n      <b>2. Condition for Motion:</b><br>\n      To drag the body forward, horizontal force must balance friction:<br>\n      F cos\theta = mu (mg - F sin\theta) = mu mg - mu F sin\theta<br>\n      F(cos\theta + mu sin\theta) = mu mg implies mathbf{F = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">mu mg</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">\\cos&theta; + mu \\sin&theta;</span>\n  </span>}.<br><br>\n      <b>3. Angle of Friction Substitution:</b><br>\n      Let lambda be the angle of friction: mu = \tanlambda = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">\\sin&lambda;</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">\\cos&lambda;</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      F = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">(\\sin&lambda; / \\cos&lambda;) mg</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">\\cos&theta; + (\\sin&lambda; / \\cos&lambda;) \\sin&theta;</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">mg \\sin&lambda;</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">\\cos&theta; \\cos&lambda; + \\sin&theta; \\sin&lambda;</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      Using the trigonometric identity cos(A - B) = cos A cos B + sin A sin B:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        F = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">mg \\sin&lambda;</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">\\cos(&theta; - &lambda;)</span>\n  </span>\n      </div>\n      <b>4. Minimization Condition:</b><br>\n      For pulling force F to be minimum, the denominator cos(\theta - lambda) must be maximum, which is +1:<br>\n      cos(\theta - lambda) = 1 implies \theta - lambda = 0 implies mathbf{\theta = lambda}.<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        F_{\\min} = mg \\sin&lambda; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">mu mg</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">&radic;(1 + mu<sup>2</sup>)</span>\n  </span>\n      </div>\n      <b>Hence, the required pulling force is strictly minimum when applied at an angle equal to the angle of friction.</b>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q95: Derive the formula for the maximum speed with which a car can safely negotiate an unbanked, level circular curve of radius r without skidding. Explain why sharp curves cannot be taken safely in wet weather.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Physical Mechanism:</b><br>\n      When an automobile navigates an unbanked flat circular curve of radius r, its velocity vector continuously rotates. This requires centripetal acceleration a_c = v<sup>2</sup>/r directed toward the center.<br>\n      The only force acting along the horizontal plane towards the center of curvature is the <b>static friction (f_s)</b> between the tire rubber and the tarmac.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Mathematical Derivation:</b><br>\n      Vertical equilibrium: N = mg.<br>\n      Required inward centripetal force: F_c = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">r</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      To prevent the vehicle from skidding outward, the required centripetal force cannot exceed the maximum available limiting friction:<br>\n      F_c le f_L = mu_s N = mu_s mg<br>\n      <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">r</span>\n  </span> le mu_s mg implies v<sup>2</sup> le mu_s r g.<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        v_{\\max} = sqrt{mu_s r g}\n      </div>\n      <b>3. Effect of Wet Weather:</b><br>\n      In dry conditions, mu_s approx 0.7 - 0.8. In wet or icy weather, a thin lubricating water film separates the tire rubber from the asphalt (hydroplaning), causing mu_s to plummet to 0.1 - 0.2.<br>\n      Since v_{\\max} propto sqrt{mu_s}, the maximum safe speed is cut by more than half. If a driver attempts to negotiate the turn above v_{\\max}, tire friction is insufficient to supply mv<sup>2</sup>/r, and the vehicle skids tangentially off the road.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q96: Derive the formula for the Optimum Speed of a vehicle on a banked road of radius r and banking angle &theta; in the absence of friction. Also derive the expression for Maximum Safe Speed when friction coefficient &mu;_s is present.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Resolution of Forces on Banked Curve:</b><br>\n      The outer edge of the road is raised by angle \theta. The normal reaction \u000bec{N} is perpendicular to the road, tilted at angle \theta to the vertical.<br><br>\n      <b>(a) Optimum Speed (v_0, Zero Friction):</b><br>\n      When moving at the engineered optimum speed v_0, no lateral frictional force is called into play (mu = 0):<br>\n      &bull; Vertical balance: N cos\theta = mg quad ldots(1)<br>\n      &bull; Horizontal centripetal force: N sin\theta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">r</span>\n  </span> quad ldots(2)<br>\n      Dividing (2) by (1):<br>\n      \tan\theta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">rg</span>\n  </span> implies mathbf{v_0 = sqrt{rg \tan\theta}}.<br><br>\n      <b>(b) Maximum Safe Speed (v_{\\max} with Friction mu_s):</b><br>\n      At maximum speed v_{\\max}, the vehicle tends to skid outward up the bank, generating limiting friction f_L = mu_s N directed downward along the banked incline.<br>\n      Resolving forces:<br>\n      &bull; Horizontally (towards center):<br>\n      N sin\theta + f_L cos\theta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v_{\\max}<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">r</span>\n  </span> implies N(sin\theta + mu_s cos\theta) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v_{\\max}<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">r</span>\n  </span> quad ldots(3)<br>\n      &bull; Vertically:<br>\n      N cos\theta - f_L sin\theta = mg implies N(cos\theta - mu_s sin\theta) = mg quad ldots(4)<br>\n      Dividing (3) by (4):<br>\n      <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v_{\\max}<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">rg</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">N(\\sin&theta; + \\mu_s\\cos&theta;)</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">N(\\cos&theta; - \\mu_s\\sin&theta;)</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      Divide numerator and denominator by cos\theta:<br>\n      <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v_{\\max}<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">rg</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">\\mu_s + \\tan&theta;</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">1 - \\mu_s \\tan&theta;</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        v_{\\max} = sqrt{rg left( <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">\\mu_s + \\tan&theta;</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">1 - \\mu_s\\tan&theta;</span>\n  </span> )}\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q97: For a vehicle negotiating a banked circular road of radius r with banking angle &theta; and friction &mu;_s, derive the expression for the Minimum Safe Speed (v_min) below which the vehicle slides down the incline.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Physical Condition for Minimum Speed:</b><br>\n      If the vehicle travels too slowly (v < v_0), the horizontal component of normal reaction Nsin\theta exceeds the required centripetal force (mv<sup>2</sup>/r). Consequently, the vehicle tends to slip downward toward the inner edge of the banked road.<br>\n      To oppose this impending downward slip, limiting friction f_L = mu_s N acts <b>upward along the incline</b>.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Mathematical Resolution:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>Horizontal equilibrium (towards center of curve):</b><br>\n      N sin\theta - f_L cos\theta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v_{\\min}<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">r</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      Substitute f_L = mu_s N:<br>\n      N(sin\theta - mu_s cos\theta) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v_{\\min}<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">r</span>\n  </span> quad ldots(1)<br><br>\n      &bull; <b>Vertical equilibrium:</b><br>\n      N cos\theta + f_L sin\theta = mg.<br>\n      Substitute f_L = mu_s N:<br>\n      N(cos\theta + mu_s sin\theta) = mg quad ldots(2)<br><br>\n      <b>3. Division and Formula Assembly:</b><br>\n      Dividing equation (1) by equation (2):<br>\n      <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v_{\\min}<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">rg</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">N(\\sin&theta; - \\mu_s\\cos&theta;)</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">N(\\cos&theta; + \\mu_s\\sin&theta;)</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      Divide numerator and denominator by cos\theta:<br>\n      <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v_{\\min}<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">rg</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">\\tan&theta; - \\mu_s</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">1 + \\mu_s \\tan&theta;</span>\n  </span>.<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        v_{\\min} = sqrt{rg left( <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">\\tan&theta; - \\mu_s</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">1 + \\mu_s\\tan&theta;</span>\n  </span> )}\n      </div>\n      If \tan\theta le mu_s, the numerator becomes zero or negative, meaning the vehicle can safely come to a complete stop (v_{\\min} = 0) without sliding down.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q98: Explain why a cyclist leans inward while taking a turn on a flat road. Derive the formula for the angle of lean &theta; with the vertical, and show that this angle is independent of the mass of the cyclist.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Physical Mechanism:</b><br>\n      A cyclist negotiating a curve of radius r at speed v requires a centripetal force mv<sup>2</sup>/r.<br>\n      If the cyclist remains upright, the ground reaction force acts vertically upward through the center of gravity, while the friction force at the wheels acts horizontally inward at ground level. These two forces create an unbalanced torque that violently tips the cyclist outward.<br>\n      To prevent toppling, the cyclist leans inward at angle \theta with the vertical. The resultant ground reaction force \u000bec{R} now tilts inward through the cyclist's center of gravity, generating zero destabilizing torque.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Derivation:</b><br>\n      Resolving the ground reaction force \u000bec{R} into rectangular components:<br>\n      &bull; Vertical component balances weight: R cos\theta = mg quad ldots(1)<br>\n      &bull; Horizontal component provides centripetal force: R sin\theta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">r</span>\n  </span> quad ldots(2)<br>\n      Dividing equation (2) by equation (1):<br>\n      <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">R \\sin&theta;</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">R \\cos&theta;</span>\n  </span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">m v<sup>2</sup> / r</span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">mg</span>\n  </span><br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        \tan\theta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">rg</span>\n  </span> implies \theta = \tan<sup>-1</sup>left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; padding: 0 4px; font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.1;\">\n    <span style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor; padding-bottom: 2px;\">v<sup>2</sup></span>\n    <span style=\"padding-top: 2px;\">rg</span>\n  </span>\right)\n      </div>\n      <b>3. Independence of Mass:</b><br>\n      The mass m cancels out entirely from the relation. Therefore, whether the rider is a lightweight child or a heavy adult, the required angle of lean depends exclusively on speed v, curve radius r, and local gravity g.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q99: A person of mass m stands on a spring scale inside an elevator. Derive the apparent weight recorded by the scale when the elevator: (a) Accelerates upward with a, (b) Accelerates downward with a, (c) Moves with uniform velocity v, (d) Falls freely under gravity (cable snaps).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Principle of Weighing Scales:</b><br>\n      A spring weighing machine does not record the true gravitational pull mg; it directly measures the normal contact reaction N exerted by the elevator floor on the occupant's feet.<br><br>\n      <b>(a) Elevator Accelerating Upward with a:</b><br>\n      Net force is upward: F_{net} = N - mg = ma.<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 6px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        N = m (g + a) = W_{true} + ma\n      </div>\n      The apparent weight is <b>greater than the true weight</b>. The passenger feels heavy.<br><br>\n      <b>(b) Elevator Accelerating Downward with a (a < g):</b><br>\n      Net force is downward: F_{net} = mg - N = ma.<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 6px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        N = m (g - a) = W_{true} - ma\n      </div>\n      The apparent weight is <b>less than the true weight</b>. The passenger feels lighter.<br><br>\n      <b>(c) Elevator Moving with Uniform Velocity (a = 0):</b><br>\n      Whether ascending or descending, when acceleration is zero: N - mg = 0.<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 6px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        N = mg = W_{true}\n      </div>\n      The apparent weight <b>equals the true weight</b>.<br><br>\n      <b>(d) Free Fall (a = g, Cable Snaps):</b><br>\n      Substituting a = g into downward formula:<br>\n      N = m (g - g) = mathbf{0}.<br>\n      The normal reaction vanishes completely. The passenger experiences total <b>weightlessness</b>, floating freely above the elevator floor.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF8A65; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q100: Master Comprehensive Synthesis of Unit III: Laws of Motion (07 Marks). Synthesize the full theoretical architecture of classical Newtonian mechanics, summarizing core laws, conservation principles, friction dynamics, circular motion constraints, and top board exam focus areas.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 138, 101, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #FF8A65; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF8A65; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Core Theoretical Architecture of Unit III:</b><br>\n      Unit III: Laws of Motion (07 Marks) represents the dynamical foundation of Newtonian mechanics, relating physical motion to the forces causing it.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Master Conceptual Pillars:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>First Law:</b> Defines force qualitatively and establishes the concept of inertia (rest, motion, direction).<br>\n      &bull; <b>Second Law:</b> Quantitative law \u000bec{F} = d\u000bec{p}/dt = m\u000bec{a}. It is the fundamental law containing both First and Third laws.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Third Law:</b> Forces occur in matched action-reaction pairs (\u000bec{F}_{AB} = -\u000bec{F}_{BA}) acting on different bodies simultaneously.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Impulse:</b> \u000bec{J} = \u000bec{F}_{avg} Delta t = Delta\u000bec{p} = \text{Area under } F-t \text{ curve}.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Momentum Conservation:</b> sum \u000bec{F}_{ext} = \u000bec{0} implies \u000bec{P} = \text{const}. Governs gun recoil (V_R = -mv/M) and rocket propulsion (F = -u , dm/dt).<br>\n      &bull; <b>Friction Dynamics:</b> Microscopic cold-welding origin. Static friction is self-adjusting (0 le f_s le mu_s N). Limiting friction f_L = mu_s N. Kinetic friction f_k = mu_k N (mu_k < mu_s). Angle of friction \tan\theta = mu_s, Angle of repose \tanalpha = mu_s implies \theta = alpha. Pulling is easier than pushing.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Circular Dynamics:</b> Centripetal force F_c = mv<sup>2</sup>/r. Unbanked road v_{\\max} = sqrt{mu_s rg}. Banked road optimum v_0 = sqrt{rg\tan\theta}, maximum v_{\\max} = sqrt{rg(\frac{mu_s+\tan\theta}{1-mu_s\tan\theta})}. Cyclist lean \tan\theta = v<sup>2</sup>/rg.<br><br>\n      <b>3. Top 5 Board Examination Trap Alerts:</b><br>\n      1. Action and reaction NEVER cancel each other out because they act on two different bodies.<br>\n      2. Normal reaction does NOT always equal mg (e.g. on inclines N = mgcos\theta, in pulling N = mg - Fsin\theta).<br>\n      3. Static friction is NOT constant; it equals applied force until limiting friction is reached.<br>\n      4. Centripetal force is NOT a new physical force; it is the label given to whichever real inward force (tension, friction, gravity, normal) maintains the curve.<br>\n      5. Caret symbols ($) are strictly forbidden in final presentation; standard superscripts must be used.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n</div>";

